window.BANK = {"index":{"curriculum_year":2026,"level":"I","mock_size":180,"seconds_per_question":90,"session_minutes":135,"session_size":90,"topics":[{"key":"ethics","name":"Ethical and Professional Standards","short":"Ethics","target":30,"weight_high":20,"weight_low":15},{"key":"fsa","name":"Financial Statement Analysis","short":"FSA","target":22,"weight_high":14,"weight_low":11},{"key":"equity","name":"Equity Investments","short":"Equity","target":22,"weight_high":14,"weight_low":11},{"key":"fi","name":"Fixed Income","short":"Fixed income","target":22,"weight_high":14,"weight_low":11},{"key":"pm","name":"Portfolio Management","short":"Portfolio","target":18,"weight_high":12,"weight_low":8},{"key":"alts","name":"Alternative Investments","short":"Alternatives","target":15,"weight_high":10,"weight_low":7},{"key":"quant","name":"Quantitative Methods","short":"Quant","target":14,"weight_high":9,"weight_low":6},{"key":"econ","name":"Economics","short":"Economics","target":13,"weight_high":9,"weight_low":6},{"key":"corporate","name":"Corporate Issuers","short":"Corporate","target":12,"weight_high":9,"weight_low":6},{"key":"deriv","name":"Derivatives","short":"Derivatives","target":12,"weight_high":8,"weight_low":5}]},"topics":{"alts":[{"answer":1,"calc":true,"choices":["14.0%","14.4%","16.0%"],"distractors":["This charges the incentive fee on the gross gain, 20% of 20.0 = 4.0, giving total fees of 6.0. The question specifies the fee is net of the management fee.","","This deducts the incentive fee only and forgets the management fee."],"id":"al-001","ref":"Hedge fund fee structure","solution":"The management fee is 2% of 100, or USD2.0 million. The gross gain is USD20.0 million, so the gain net of the management fee is USD18.0 million and the incentive fee is 20% of that, USD3.6 million. Total fees are USD5.6 million, leaving USD114.4 million and a net return of 14.4%.","stem":"A fund begins the year with USD100 million and earns a 20% gross return. It charges a 2% management fee on beginning-of-period assets and a 20% incentive fee calculated on the return net of the management fee. There is no hurdle rate. The investor's net return for the year is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":true,"choices":["USD11.7 million","USD27.7 million","USD117 million"],"distractors":["This multiplies rather than divides, 1.80 x 6.5.","","This divides by 0.0065 rather than 0.065, a decimal place error of one order of magnitude."],"id":"al-002","ref":"Direct capitalisation","solution":"Direct capitalisation divides net operating income by the capitalisation rate: 1.80 / 0.065 = USD27.7 million. The capitalisation rate behaves like the inverse of a multiple, so a lower rate implies a higher value.","stem":"An office building generates net operating income of USD1.80 million a year. Comparable transactions imply a capitalisation rate of 6.5%. Using direct capitalisation, the value of the building is closest to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["the J-curve effect.","carried interest.","a clawback provision."],"distractors":["","Carried interest is the general partner's share of profits, typically 20%. It contributes to the early drag but is not the name of the pattern.","A clawback obliges the general partner to return incentive fees already taken where later losses mean it was overpaid across the fund's life."],"id":"al-003","ref":"The J-curve","solution":"Fees and expenses are charged from the outset while portfolio companies are still held at or near cost, so early reported returns are negative. Returns turn positive as value is created and exits occur, tracing a J shape through the fund's life.","stem":"A private equity fund reports negative returns in its first few years before turning positive as investments are realised. This pattern is best described as:"},{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["1.00 per unit","5.00 per unit","0.00 per unit"],"distractors":["","This charges 20% of the full 25 point recovery, which would have investors paying twice for the same ground.","There is a fee, because the unit finished above the previous peak. It would be nil had the recovery stopped at or below 100."],"id":"al-004","ref":"High water mark","solution":"A high water mark means the fee applies only to gains above the previous peak of 100. The unit rose from 80 to 105, but only the 5 above the high water mark is fee-bearing: 20% of 5.00 = 1.00 per unit.","stem":"A fund charges a 20% incentive fee subject to a high water mark, and no management fee. Net asset value per unit falls from 100 to 80 in one year, then recovers to 105 the next. The incentive fee charged in the second year is closest to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["fees are levied at two levels, so total costs are materially higher.","the incentive fee is lower, so total costs are materially lower.","costs are similar, because the outer layer replaces rather than adds to the inner fees."],"distractors":["","The outer incentive fee is lower in isolation, but it does not replace the underlying managers' fees.","The outer layer is additional, not substitutional. That is the defining feature of the structure."],"id":"al-005","ref":"Fund of funds fees","solution":"The fund of funds fee sits on top of what the underlying managers charge, so the investor pays both layers. Diversification and manager selection are what the second layer buys, and they have to be worth the drag.","stem":"An investor compares a single hedge fund charging 2 and 20 with a fund of hedge funds charging 1 and 10 on top of the same underlying managers. The main consequence of the fund of funds structure is most likely that:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["allow illiquid positions to be held without being forced to sell into a redemption.","guarantee investors a minimum return over the lock-up period.","reduce the management fee by lowering administrative costs."],"distractors":["","Nothing about a lock-up guarantees a return. It restricts the investor's exit, which is the opposite of a protection.","Administrative savings are trivial next to the liquidity mismatch these terms exist to manage."],"id":"al-006","ref":"Lock-ups and redemption terms","solution":"Redemption terms match the liquidity the fund offers to the liquidity of what it holds. Without them a strategy in less liquid instruments would face forced selling exactly when prices are worst, which harms the investors who stay as much as the manager.","stem":"A hedge fund imposes a two-year lock-up and requires 90 days notice for redemptions thereafter. From the manager's perspective, the main purpose of these terms is most likely to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["venture capital.","a leveraged buyout.","a distressed debt strategy."],"distractors":["Venture capital funds early stage companies that have little or no cash flow and cannot support debt, and it accepts a high failure rate.","","A distressed strategy buys the obligations of companies already in difficulty, often to influence a restructuring rather than to run the business."],"id":"al-007","ref":"Private equity strategies","solution":"A leveraged buyout takes a mature business with predictable cash flows, which is what supports the borrowing, and seeks returns from debt paydown, operational improvement and exit multiple expansion.","stem":"A fund acquires established, cash generative businesses using substantial debt, then improves operations before exiting. This strategy is best described as:"},{"answer":1,"calc":true,"choices":["USD3 million","USD8 million","USD13 million"],"distractors":["This nets the distributions against the commitment, which confuses cash returned with capital still owed.","","This adds the distributions back to the unfunded amount."],"id":"al-008","ref":"Committed and drawn capital","solution":"The unfunded commitment is what has been promised but not yet called: 20 - 12 = USD8 million. Distributions received do not reduce the commitment, and the investor must keep the USD8 million available for future capital calls.","stem":"An investor commits USD20 million to a private equity fund. Three years in, the fund has called USD12 million and distributed USD5 million. The investor's remaining unfunded commitment is closest to:"},{"answer":2,"calc":true,"choices":["USD0.9m","USD1.3m","USD1.9m"],"distractors":["This deducts interest and depreciation as well, which is closer to a taxable income figure than to net operating income.","This deducts the interest but not depreciation.",""],"id":"al-009","ref":"Net operating income","solution":"Net operating income is income after vacancy and operating expenses but before financing and depreciation: 3.0 - 0.2 - 0.9 = USD1.9m. Excluding interest is what makes the measure independent of how the property is funded.","stem":"A property generates gross rental income of USD3.0m, with vacancy losses of USD0.2m, operating expenses of USD0.9m, interest on the mortgage of USD0.6m and depreciation of USD0.4m. Net operating income is closest to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["greater liquidity, and a price that can diverge from the value of the underlying assets.","greater liquidity, and a price that tracks appraised asset value closely by construction.","less liquidity, but more precise exposure to a chosen property."],"distractors":["","Nothing forces the share price to appraised value. Persistent discounts are common.","A listed trust is more liquid, not less. Precise exposure to one building is an argument for direct ownership."],"id":"al-010","ref":"REITs and direct property","solution":"A listed trust trades daily, which is its main advantage over direct ownership, but the share price is set by the equity market and can sit at a substantial premium or discount to the appraised value of the portfolio.","stem":"Compared with owning commercial property directly, holding a listed real estate investment trust most likely offers:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["spot price return, roll return and collateral return.","spot price return and dividend income.","spot price return and the convenience yield."],"distractors":["","Commodities pay no dividend, which is one reason the return decomposition differs from equities.","Convenience yield helps explain the shape of the futures curve rather than being a return the investor receives directly."],"id":"al-011","ref":"Components of commodity futures return","solution":"A futures position earns the change in the price of the contract held, a roll return as each expiring contract is replaced by a later one, and interest on the collateral posted. Roll return is the component investors most often overlook.","stem":"The total return on a fully collateralised long position in commodity futures is most accurately described as the sum of:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["positive.","negative.","zero."],"distractors":["A positive roll return comes from backwardation, where the curve slopes downward.","","The roll return is zero only where the curve is flat across the rolled maturities."],"id":"al-012","ref":"Contango and roll return","solution":"Contango means longer-dated contracts cost more than nearer ones, so each roll sells a cheaper expiring contract and buys a dearer one. That drag is a negative roll return, and over time it can overwhelm a flat spot price.","stem":"A commodity futures curve is in contango. For an investor holding a long position and rolling it forward each month, the roll return will most likely be:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["higher construction and demand risk, with cash flows further in the future.","lower risk, because no operating history has to be improved on.","similar risk, since both are regulated assets."],"distractors":["","Absence of an operating history is a source of uncertainty, not comfort.","Regulation shapes the return but does not equalise construction risk between a project that exists and one that does not."],"id":"al-013","ref":"Infrastructure investment","solution":"Greenfield means building the asset, so the investor takes construction, cost overrun and untested demand risk before any revenue arrives. Brownfield assets already operate, with a revenue history to underwrite against.","stem":"Compared with a brownfield infrastructure investment, a greenfield project most likely carries:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["higher measured volatility and higher correlation with equities.","lower measured volatility and lower correlation with equities.","similar volatility, since both reflect the same underlying assets."],"distractors":["The smoothing works the other way: it dampens the reported swings rather than amplifying them.","","The underlying assets may be similar, but how they are valued changes what the return series looks like, which is the point."],"id":"al-014","ref":"Appraisal-based valuation","solution":"Appraisals are infrequent and lag the market, which smooths the series and understates both volatility and correlation with traded assets. The diversification such a fund appears to offer is therefore partly a measurement artefact.","stem":"Returns for an unlisted property fund are calculated from periodic independent appraisals rather than transaction prices. Compared with a listed equivalent, the reported return series will most likely show:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["survivorship and backfill bias.","the inclusion of management and incentive fees.","the use of monthly rather than daily pricing."],"distractors":["","Fees reduce reported returns rather than overstating them.","Monthly pricing affects measured volatility and the timing of reported returns rather than biasing the level upward."],"id":"al-015","ref":"Hedge fund index biases","solution":"Managers who close after poor results drop out of the index, and those who join often bring a favourable history with them. Both effects push reported index returns above what an investor could have earned selecting managers in advance.","stem":"Reported returns on a hedge fund index built from voluntary manager submissions are most likely to be overstated because of:"}],"corporate":[{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["8.42%","8.90%","9.20%"],"distractors":["","This tax adjusts both components, applying (1 - t) to the cost of equity as well. Dividends are not deductible, so only debt earns the shield.","This omits the tax shield entirely: 0.40 x 6.5% + 0.60 x 11.0% = 9.20%."],"id":"ci-001","ref":"Weighted average cost of capital","solution":"Only the debt cost is tax adjusted: 6.5% x (1 - 0.30) = 4.55%. Then 0.40 x 4.55% + 0.60 x 11.0% = 1.82% + 6.60% = 8.42%.","stem":"A company funds itself with 40% debt and 60% equity at market values. Its before-tax cost of debt is 6.5%, its cost of equity is 11.0%, and its marginal tax rate is 30%. Its weighted average cost of capital is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["choose the project with the higher internal rate of return, because it earns more per unit of capital.","choose the project with the higher net present value, because it adds more value in currency terms.","reject both, because a conflict indicates the cash flow estimates are unreliable."],"distractors":["A high rate on a small base can add less value than a lower rate on a large base. IRR also implicitly assumes reinvestment at the IRR itself.","","A ranking conflict between the two methods is an ordinary consequence of differing scale or timing, not evidence that the forecasts are wrong."],"id":"ci-002","ref":"NPV against IRR","solution":"Net present value measures the currency amount of value added, which is what shareholder wealth is denominated in. IRR is a rate and is blind to scale, so a small project with a high rate can beat a large project that creates far more value. Where the two conflict, NPV governs.","stem":"Two mutually exclusive projects of very different scale rank differently under net present value and internal rate of return. For a company whose objective is to maximise shareholder wealth, the most appropriate decision rule is to:"},{"answer":2,"calc":true,"choices":["0.40","1.50","2.50"],"distractors":["This is the reciprocal, operating income over contribution.","This is fixed cost divided by operating income, 600,000 / 400,000. Leverage compares contribution with operating income, not fixed cost with it.",""],"id":"ci-003","ref":"Degree of operating leverage","solution":"Contribution margin is (25.00 - 15.00) x 100,000 = USD1,000,000. Operating income is 1,000,000 - 600,000 = USD400,000. Degree of operating leverage is contribution divided by operating income: 1,000,000 / 400,000 = 2.50. A 1% change in units moves operating income by about 2.5%.","stem":"A manufacturer sells 100,000 units at USD25.00 each. Variable cost is USD15.00 per unit and fixed operating cost is USD600,000. Its degree of operating leverage at this level of output is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":true,"choices":["negative USD31,000","positive USD31,000","positive USD200,000"],"distractors":["This has the sign the wrong way round. The discounted inflows exceed the outlay.","","This is the undiscounted sum of the cash flows less the outlay, 1,200,000 - 1,000,000, which ignores the time value of money."],"id":"ci-004","ref":"Net present value","solution":"The three-year annuity factor at 8.0% is 2.5771, so the present value of the inflows is 400,000 x 2.5771 = USD1,030,800. Net of the outlay, the net present value is about positive USD31,000, so the project adds value.","stem":"A project requires an outlay of USD1,000,000 today and is expected to generate USD400,000 at the end of each of the next three years. At a cost of capital of 8.0%, its net present value is closest to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["ignores the time value of money and all cash flows after the payback point.","cannot be calculated where cash flows are uneven.","always produces the same ranking as net present value."],"distractors":["","Uneven cash flows are handled easily by accumulating them until the outlay is recovered.","The two criteria frequently disagree, which is the point of the criticism."],"id":"ci-005","ref":"The payback period","solution":"Payback treats a currency unit received in year four as equal to one received today, and it stops counting once the outlay is recovered. A long-lived project with strong later cash flows can therefore be rejected in favour of a short-lived one.","stem":"The main shortcoming of the payback period as a capital budgeting criterion is most likely that it:"},{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["8.00%","12.50%","6.25%"],"distractors":["","This divides the price by the dividend and misplaces the decimal, or otherwise inverts the ratio.","This divides the dividend by 80, or applies the dividend to a different price."],"id":"ci-006","ref":"Cost of preferred equity","solution":"For a perpetual preferred share the cost is the dividend divided by the price: 5.00 / 62.50 = 8.00%. There is no growth term, because the dividend is fixed.","stem":"A perpetual preferred share pays an annual dividend of USD5.00 and trades at USD62.50. The cost of this preferred equity is closest to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["75 days","105 days","135 days"],"distractors":["","This omits the payables benefit. Supplier credit shortens the cycle because the company holds the cash for longer.","This adds all three figures rather than deducting payables."],"id":"ci-007","ref":"Cash conversion cycle","solution":"The cash conversion cycle is days of inventory plus days sales outstanding less days payables outstanding: 60 + 45 - 30 = 75 days. It measures how long cash is tied up between paying suppliers and collecting from customers.","stem":"A company reports days sales outstanding of 45, days of inventory on hand of 60 and days payables outstanding of 30. Its cash conversion cycle is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["reduce the weighted average cost of capital, because debt is cheaper than equity.","leave the weighted average cost of capital unchanged, as the cost of equity rises to offset it.","increase the weighted average cost of capital, because financial risk rises."],"distractors":["This is the intuition the propositions were written to correct. It ignores what leverage does to the cost of equity.","","Financial risk does rise, but it is borne by equity holders and shows up in their required return rather than in the overall cost of capital."],"id":"ci-008","ref":"Capital structure without taxes","solution":"Adding debt does put more weight on the cheaper source, but it also concentrates the business risk onto a smaller equity base, so the cost of equity rises by exactly enough to offset it. The overall cost of capital, and the value of the firm, are unchanged.","stem":"Under the Modigliani and Miller propositions in a world with no taxes and no bankruptcy costs, an increase in a company's use of debt will most likely:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["shareholders' total wealth unchanged, absent taxes and signalling effects.","shareholders better off, because the share price must rise.","shareholders worse off, because they receive no cash."],"distractors":["","Earnings per share may rise, but the company is also smaller by the cash spent. There is no free gain in the exchange.","Holders who want cash can sell into the buyback, so no one is deprived of liquidity."],"id":"ci-009","ref":"Dividends and share repurchases","solution":"Both routes return the same cash. A dividend pays every holder directly, while a repurchase pays only the sellers and leaves the remaining holders with a larger share of a smaller company. Ignoring taxes and any signal, total wealth is the same either way.","stem":"A company with surplus cash chooses a share repurchase over a special dividend of the same amount. Compared with the dividend, the repurchase will most likely leave:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["commercial paper issued into the money market.","a drawn bank term loan with a five-year maturity.","issuing new equity."],"distractors":["","A five-year loan funds a few weeks of need for years, and carries a higher rate for the term the company does not require.","Equity is the most expensive form of funding, and issuing it to cover a seasonal swing is permanent capital for a temporary need."],"id":"ci-010","ref":"Short-term funding sources","solution":"Commercial paper is short-dated unsecured funding available to highly rated issuers at rates close to the money market, which matches the tenor of a seasonal need. Rating matters because weaker issuers cannot access the market on attractive terms.","stem":"A company with a strong investment grade rating needs to fund a seasonal working capital swing at the lowest cost. Its most appropriate source is most likely:"},{"answer":2,"calc":true,"choices":["8.0%","13.0%","20.0%"],"distractors":["This applies financial leverage alone, 5% x 1.60.","This adds the two leverage measures rather than multiplying them.",""],"id":"ci-011","ref":"Degree of total leverage","solution":"Total leverage is the product of the two: 2.50 x 1.60 = 4.00. A 5% rise in units therefore lifts net income by about 5% x 4.00 = 20%. Leverage multiplies in both directions, which is why the same structure is dangerous on the way down.","stem":"A company has a degree of operating leverage of 2.50 and a degree of financial leverage of 1.60. A 5% increase in unit sales will most likely change net income by closest to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["the board lacks the independence to challenge management on behalf of shareholders.","the board will lack the operational knowledge to make informed decisions.","executive remuneration will be set too low to retain talent."],"distractors":["","Operational knowledge is the one thing such a board would have in abundance. Independence is what it lacks.","The risk runs the other way: a board dominated by executives is more likely to approve generous pay."],"id":"ci-012","ref":"Corporate governance","solution":"The board's central function is to hold management to account. A board drawn from management, chaired by the chief executive, is being asked to supervise itself, which is why codes call for a majority of independent directors and separation of the chair and chief executive roles.","stem":"A board is composed almost entirely of executives, with the chief executive also serving as chair. The governance concern this most likely raises is that:"}],"deriv":[{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["4.15","8.00","11.85"],"distractors":["","This assumes the put and call must be equal at the money. They are equal only when the strike carries no time value of money, that is at a zero interest rate.","This adds the present value discount rather than subtracting it, 8.00 + 100.00 - 96.15."],"id":"dv-001","ref":"Put call parity","solution":"Put call parity states that the call less the put equals the share price less the present value of the strike. So the put equals 8.00 - 100.00 + 100.00 / 1.04 = 8.00 - 100.00 + 96.15 = USD4.15. The put is cheaper than the call because the strike is discounted.","stem":"A European call on a non-dividend-paying share with a strike of USD100 and one year to expiry trades at USD8.00. The share trades at USD100 and the one-year risk-free rate is 4.0%. The price of the European put with the same strike and expiry is closest to:"},{"answer":2,"calc":true,"choices":["1,425","1,500","1,575"],"distractors":["This discounts the spot rather than compounding it, which is the relationship between a forward price and its present value, not the forward price itself.","The forward equals the spot only at a zero interest rate, or where a convenience yield exactly offsets the financing cost.",""],"id":"dv-002","ref":"Forward price with no carry","solution":"With no carry, the forward price is the spot compounded at the risk-free rate: 1,500 x 1.05 = USD1,575. Any other price allows a riskless profit by borrowing, buying the asset and selling the forward.","stem":"An asset with no storage cost, no convenience yield and no cash flows trades at USD1,500. The one-year risk-free rate is 5.0%. The no-arbitrage one-year forward price is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["greater counterparty credit exposure.","daily settlement of gains and losses.","terms customised to the two counterparties."],"distractors":["The opposite holds. Central clearing and daily margining make credit exposure smaller, not greater.","","Customisation is the advantage of a forward. Futures are standardised as to size, quality and delivery date so they can trade on an exchange."],"id":"dv-003","ref":"Futures against forwards","solution":"Futures are marked to market daily through a clearing house, with variation margin passing between accounts each day. That daily settlement, together with the clearing house standing between the parties, is what reduces credit exposure relative to a forward.","stem":"Compared with an otherwise equivalent forward contract, an exchange-traded futures contract is most likely to feature:"},{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["USD2.00","USD6.00","USD8.00"],"distractors":["","This is the intrinsic value rather than the time value.","This is the whole premium, which would be time value only for an option with no intrinsic value."],"id":"dv-004","ref":"Intrinsic value and time value","solution":"Intrinsic value is the payoff from immediate exercise, 56 - 50 = USD6.00. The premium is USD8.00, so the remaining USD2.00 is time value, which decays to zero at expiry.","stem":"A call option with a strike of USD50 trades at a premium of USD8.00 when the underlying share is at USD56. The time value of the option is closest to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["USD37.00","USD40.00","USD43.00"],"distractors":["","At the strike the put expires worthless and the investor has lost the full premium.","This adds the premium to the strike, which is the breakeven on a long call rather than a long put."],"id":"dv-005","ref":"Breakeven on a long put","solution":"The put pays 40 less the share price at expiry, and the investor paid 3.00 for it, so the position breaks even when the share is at 37.00. Below that the put is profitable, and the maximum loss is the premium.","stem":"An investor buys a put with a strike of USD40 for a premium of USD3.00. Ignoring transaction costs, the share price at expiry at which the position breaks even is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["a higher maximum gain and a lower maximum loss.","a capped maximum gain and a maximum loss reduced by the premium.","an unlimited maximum gain and an unchanged maximum loss."],"distractors":["The maximum gain is lower, not higher, because the upside above the strike belongs to the option buyer.","","Selling the call removes the unlimited upside, which is the defining feature of the strategy."],"id":"dv-006","ref":"Covered call","solution":"Writing the call sells away the upside beyond the strike, so the gain is capped there plus the premium received. The premium also cushions a fall, so the maximum loss is the share price less the premium. It is a trade of upside for income.","stem":"An investor who owns a share writes a call on it with a strike above the current price. Compared with holding the share alone, the position most likely has:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["buying insurance on the holding, with the premium as the cost of cover.","selling the share and buying a call, which requires no premium.","a risk-free position, because the downside is eliminated."],"distractors":["","Holding cash plus a call does replicate the payoff shape, but the call itself costs a premium, so it is not free.","The downside is floored at the strike, not eliminated, and the premium itself is at risk."],"id":"dv-007","ref":"Protective put","solution":"The put sets a floor on the value of the holding, and the premium is what the floor costs. Like any insurance it reduces expected return by the premium in exchange for removing the tail.","stem":"An investor holding a share buys a put on it with a strike near the current price. The position is best described as economically equivalent to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["a gain on the swap, offsetting the higher cost of the underlying debt.","a loss on the swap, compounding the higher cost of the underlying debt.","no change, because a swap has zero value at inception."],"distractors":["","This describes a receive-fixed position, which is exposed to rising rates rather than protected from them.","Zero value applies at inception only. The swap acquires value as rates move away from the fixed rate struck."],"id":"dv-008","ref":"Interest rate swaps","solution":"Paying fixed and receiving floating gains value as rates rise, because the floating leg received is worth more against a fixed leg that does not move. That gain is the point of the hedge: it offsets the increased interest on the floating rate debt.","stem":"A borrower with floating rate debt enters a swap to pay fixed and receive floating on the same notional. If reference rates subsequently rise, the borrower's position will most likely show:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["zero, because the present values of the two legs are equal.","positive to the fixed rate payer, who takes on more risk.","equal to the notional multiplied by the fixed rate."],"distractors":["","Neither side has an advantage at inception, which is what setting the rate at par achieves.","The notional is never exchanged on an interest rate swap. It is a reference amount for calculating the payments."],"id":"dv-009","ref":"Swap value at inception","solution":"The par swap rate is the fixed rate that makes the present value of the fixed leg equal to the present value of the expected floating leg, so neither side pays the other at the outset. Any departure from that rate is settled with an upfront payment.","stem":"The fixed rate on a newly agreed interest rate swap is set so that the value of the contract at inception is most likely:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["has the right but not the obligation to transact, and pays a premium for it.","has the obligation to transact, but at a better price.","faces greater downside, because options expire worthless."],"distractors":["","The obligation belongs to the forward. An option buyer is never compelled to exercise.","The option buyer's loss is limited to the premium, whereas the forward buyer's loss is unbounded as the price falls."],"id":"dv-010","ref":"Forwards against options","solution":"A forward commits both parties, and costs nothing at inception. An option gives its buyer a choice, which is why it has to be paid for. The premium is the price of being able to walk away.","stem":"The difference between buying a forward and buying a call option on the same underlying is most likely that the option buyer:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["the issuer's credit quality deteriorates and spreads widen.","the issuer's credit quality improves and spreads tighten.","risk-free interest rates rise while spreads are unchanged."],"distractors":["","Tightening spreads make the protection worth less than the premium being paid, which is a loss to the buyer.","A credit default swap is designed to isolate credit risk from interest rate risk, so a move in risk-free rates alone has little effect."],"id":"dv-011","ref":"Credit default swaps","solution":"The protection buyer pays a periodic premium and receives a payout on a credit event, so the position behaves like a short position in the issuer's credit. Widening spreads mean the protection now costs more than the buyer is paying, which is a gain.","stem":"An investor buys protection on a corporate issuer through a credit default swap. The position will most likely gain in value if:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["increase the value of a call and reduce the value of a put.","increase the value of both a call and a put.","reduce the value of both a call and a put."],"distractors":["This treats volatility as directional. It is not: the asymmetry of the payoff means dispersion helps both.","","Higher volatility never reduces option value, because the downside is already truncated at the premium paid."],"id":"dv-012","ref":"Option value and volatility","solution":"An option holder gains from favourable moves and can walk away from unfavourable ones, so a wider distribution of outcomes is worth more whichever direction the option points. Volatility raises call and put values alike.","stem":"Holding everything else constant, an increase in the volatility of the underlying asset will most likely:"}],"econ":[{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["95.59","229.77","0.0044"],"distractors":["","This divides rather than multiplies, 148.20 / 0.6450, which is the AUD/JPY relationship inverted and gives an implausible number of yen per Australian dollar.","This is the reciprocal, AUD per yen, which is the quote the question did not ask for."],"id":"ec-001","ref":"Cross rates","solution":"The USD cancels when the quotes are multiplied in the right order: JPY/AUD = JPY/USD multiplied by USD/AUD = 148.20 x 0.6450 = 95.59. One Australian dollar buys about 96 yen.","stem":"A dealer quotes USD/AUD 0.6450 and JPY/USD 148.20, where each quote states the price currency first. The JPY/AUD cross rate is closest to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["increase.","decrease.","remain unchanged."],"distractors":["","Revenue falls on a price rise only where demand is elastic, that is where the elasticity is more negative than -1.","Revenue is unchanged on a price rise only at unit elasticity, an elasticity of exactly -1."],"id":"ec-002","ref":"Price elasticity and total revenue","solution":"An elasticity between zero and -1 means demand is inelastic: quantity falls proportionally less than the price rises, here about 6% against 10%. The price effect dominates the volume effect, so total revenue increases.","stem":"The own-price elasticity of demand for a product is estimated at -0.6. If the producer raises the price by 10%, total revenue will most likely:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["appreciate, because higher prices attract capital seeking higher nominal returns.","depreciate, by approximately the inflation differential.","remain stable, because relative prices adjust rather than the exchange rate."],"distractors":["This confuses nominal with real returns. Higher inflation does not deliver a higher real return, and the Fisher relation predicts the nominal interest premium is offset by depreciation.","","Under purchasing power parity it is the nominal exchange rate that adjusts. A stable rate would leave the real exchange rate drifting indefinitely."],"id":"ec-003","ref":"Relative purchasing power parity","solution":"Relative purchasing power parity holds that the change in a spot exchange rate approximates the difference in inflation rates. The higher-inflation currency loses purchasing power at home and so must fall against the lower-inflation currency to keep tradeable goods prices aligned.","stem":"Over the long run and holding other influences constant, the currency of a country whose inflation rate is persistently higher than its trading partner's will most likely:"},{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["USD0.6344 per AUD","USD0.6500 per AUD","USD0.6659 per AUD"],"distractors":["","The forward equals the spot only where the two interest rates are equal.","This applies the ratio the wrong way up, 0.6500 x (1.04 / 1.015), which would have the high-yielding currency at a forward premium and leave a riskless profit on the table."],"id":"ec-004","ref":"Covered interest rate parity","solution":"The forward adjusts by the ratio of the two interest factors: 0.6500 x (1.015 / 1.04) = USD0.6344. The higher-yielding currency trades at a forward discount, which is exactly what removes the arbitrage from borrowing in the low-rate currency and lending in the high-rate one.","stem":"The spot rate is USD0.6500 per AUD. One-year interest rates are 4.00% in Australian dollars and 1.50% in US dollars. The one-year forward rate consistent with covered interest rate parity is closest to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["1,030","1,060","1,450"],"distractors":["","This adds net exports with the sign reversed, treating the trade deficit as an addition.","This adds imports to the other components rather than subtracting them."],"id":"ec-005","ref":"GDP by the expenditure approach","solution":"GDP is consumption plus investment plus government spending plus net exports: 620 + 190 + 250 + (180 - 210) = 1,030. Imports are deducted because they were produced elsewhere.","stem":"An economy reports consumption of 620, gross investment of 190, government spending on goods and services of 250, exports of 180 and imports of 210. GDP by the expenditure approach is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":true,"choices":["90.9","110.0","150.0"],"distractors":["This inverts the ratio, dividing real by nominal.","","This treats the difference between the two figures as the index level."],"id":"ec-006","ref":"The GDP deflator","solution":"The deflator is nominal GDP divided by real GDP, times 100: 550 / 500 x 100 = 110.0. Prices have risen 10% since the base year.","stem":"Nominal GDP is 550 and real GDP measured in base-year prices is 500. The GDP deflator is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["unemployed, because they want to work.","outside the labour force, so the unemployment rate does not capture them.","underemployed, and included in the unemployment rate at a partial weight."],"distractors":["Wanting work is not sufficient. Active search is the test.","","Underemployment describes people working fewer hours or below their skill level. It is measured separately and not as a fraction of the unemployment rate."],"id":"ec-007","ref":"Labour market measurement","solution":"The unemployment rate counts only those actively seeking work. A discouraged worker who has stopped searching drops out of the labour force entirely, which is why the headline rate can fall for reasons that are not good news.","stem":"A worker has given up looking for a job after a long unsuccessful search but would take one if offered. In official statistics this person is most likely counted as:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["substitute toward goods whose prices have risen less.","buy more of every good as incomes rise.","delay purchases when prices rise."],"distractors":["","Rising incomes change the level of spending rather than creating the substitution bias in the index.","Deferring purchases affects the timing of demand, not the measured price of the fixed basket."],"id":"ec-008","ref":"Bias in consumer price indices","solution":"A fixed basket assumes buying patterns do not change, but households shift away from what has become relatively expensive. Ignoring that substitution overstates the cost of maintaining a given standard of living.","stem":"A consumer price index measured against a fixed basket of goods will most likely overstate inflation because consumers:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["the exchange rate, which lowers the cost of imports.","borrowing costs on mortgages and consumer credit.","government bond issuance, which absorbs private savings."],"distractors":["A stronger currency does tighten conditions, but it works through traded goods prices and exporters rather than directly on household budgets.","","Bond issuance is a fiscal decision. It is not a monetary policy transmission channel."],"id":"ec-009","ref":"Monetary policy transmission","solution":"Higher policy rates feed through to lending rates, which raises debt service and reduces the capacity and appetite to borrow. In heavily mortgaged economies this is the fastest and largest channel to household demand.","stem":"A central bank raises its policy rate to slow demand. The transmission channel that operates most directly on household spending is a rise in:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["discretionary fiscal stimulus.","automatic stabilisers.","monetary accommodation."],"distractors":["Discretionary stimulus requires a deliberate new decision, which is what this scenario excludes.","","These are fiscal flows through the budget, not central bank actions."],"id":"ec-010","ref":"Automatic stabilisers","solution":"Automatic stabilisers are features of the existing tax and transfer system that cushion demand as the cycle turns, with no decision required. They act faster than discretionary measures precisely because nothing has to be legislated.","stem":"During a recession, government tax receipts fall and unemployment benefit payments rise without any new legislation. These effects are best described as:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["entering a downturn, as sales fall faster than production is cut.","entering an expansion, as firms build stock ahead of demand.","at trend, since the ratio is stable over the cycle."],"distractors":["","Deliberate stockbuilding does happen, but at a turning point the ratio rises because the denominator has fallen, which is the more common reading.","The ratio is distinctly cyclical, which is why it is watched."],"id":"ec-011","ref":"Business cycle indicators","solution":"A rising inventory to sales ratio usually reflects unsold output rather than deliberate stockbuilding, because production plans lag a fall in demand. Firms then cut output to clear stock, which deepens the downturn.","stem":"An analyst observes that the ratio of inventories to sales has risen sharply across manufacturers. This is most consistent with an economy:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["positive, sustained by cost advantages.","zero, as entry drives price to the minimum of average total cost.","negative, because it has no pricing power."],"distractors":["Sustained cost advantage would imply differentiation or a barrier, neither of which exists in this structure.","","Zero economic profit is not a loss. It means the firm earns exactly its opportunity cost of capital."],"id":"ec-012","ref":"Market structure","solution":"A horizontal demand curve and an identical product describe perfect competition. Free entry means any economic profit attracts capacity until price equals minimum average total cost, leaving zero economic profit though the firm still earns its cost of capital.","stem":"A firm faces a horizontal demand curve at the prevailing market price and sells an identical product to many others. Its long-run economic profit is most likely:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["impossible, because Country A is more efficient at everything.","possible, provided their opportunity costs of the two goods differ.","possible only if Country B subsidises its producers."],"distractors":["This is the absolute advantage argument the theory of comparative advantage displaced.","","Subsidies distort the pattern of trade rather than creating the gains, which arise from differing opportunity costs."],"id":"ec-013","ref":"Comparative advantage","solution":"Absolute advantage in both goods does not preclude gains from trade. What matters is the ratio at which each country gives up one good to produce the other, and where those opportunity costs differ both countries gain by specialising in the good they sacrifice least to make.","stem":"Country A can produce both wheat and cloth using fewer resources than Country B. According to the theory of comparative advantage, mutually beneficial trade between them is most likely:"}],"equity":[{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["42.00","43.68","52.50"],"distractors":["","This grows the dividend a second time, 2.10 x 1.04 / 0.05, treating the stated figure as the dividend just paid rather than the one expected next year.","This divides by the growth rate alone, 2.10 / 0.04, omitting the required return."],"id":"eq-001","ref":"Gordon growth model","solution":"The constant growth model divides the next dividend by the required return less the growth rate: 2.10 / (0.09 - 0.04) = 2.10 / 0.05 = USD42.00. The dividend given is already the year-one figure, so it needs no further growing.","stem":"A share is expected to pay a dividend of USD2.10 one year from today. The required return on equity is 9.0% and dividends are expected to grow indefinitely at 4.0%. The intrinsic value of the share is closest to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["price weighted.","market capitalisation weighted.","equal weighted."],"distractors":["","Market capitalisation is unchanged by a split, so no adjustment is needed.","An equal weighted index assigns weights independently of price, so a split does not disturb it either. Equal weighting requires frequent rebalancing for a different reason, namely price drift."],"id":"eq-002","ref":"Index weighting methods","solution":"A price weighted index sums constituent prices and divides by a divisor, so halving a price would cut the index level even though no value has been created or destroyed. The divisor is adjusted to keep the index continuous. A capitalisation weighted index is unaffected, because the share count doubles as the price halves.","stem":"An index constituent undertakes a two-for-one stock split. The index construction method that requires the index divisor to be adjusted as a direct result is most likely:"},{"answer":1,"calc":true,"choices":["8.00","8.40","10.50"],"distractors":["This is the justified leading multiple, 0.40 / 0.05, which applies to next year's earnings rather than the trailing figure.","","This uses the retention ratio of 0.60 in place of the payout ratio, less the growth adjustment."],"id":"eq-003","ref":"Justified trailing price to earnings","solution":"The payout ratio is 1 - 0.60 = 0.40. The justified trailing multiple is payout multiplied by one plus the growth rate, divided by the required return less growth: 0.40 x 1.05 / (0.10 - 0.05) = 0.42 / 0.05 = 8.40.","stem":"A company retains 60% of its earnings, has a required return on equity of 10.0%, and is expected to grow at 5.0% indefinitely. Its justified trailing price to earnings ratio is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":true,"choices":["6.0%","9.0%","25.0%"],"distractors":["This uses the payout ratio of 40% rather than the retention ratio. Growth comes from the earnings kept, not the earnings paid out.","","This divides return on equity by the retention ratio rather than multiplying."],"id":"eq-004","ref":"Sustainable growth rate","solution":"The sustainable growth rate is the retention ratio multiplied by return on equity: 0.60 x 15.0% = 9.0%. It is the rate at which equity, and therefore earnings, can compound without raising new capital or changing leverage.","stem":"A company earns a return on equity of 15.0% and retains 60% of its earnings. Assuming no change in leverage or margins, its sustainable growth rate is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":true,"choices":["1.4","1.8","2.8"],"distractors":["This is return on equity divided by the required return, which ignores growth on both sides.","","This divides return on equity by the spread between the required return and growth, mixing the two formulations."],"id":"eq-005","ref":"Justified price to book","solution":"The justified price to book ratio is (return on equity less growth) divided by (required return less growth): (0.14 - 0.05) / (0.10 - 0.05) = 0.09 / 0.05 = 1.8. A company earning above its cost of equity is worth more than its book value.","stem":"A bank is expected to earn a sustainable return on equity of 14.0%, grow at 5.0% indefinitely, and its investors require 10.0%. Its justified price to book ratio is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":true,"choices":["USD25.00","USD33.33","USD37.50"],"distractors":["This is the loan amount per share, which is the price at which equity reaches zero rather than 25%.","","This is a 25% fall from the purchase price, which confuses the size of the price decline with the maintenance margin percentage."],"id":"eq-006","ref":"Margin call price","solution":"The investor puts up USD25,000 and borrows USD25,000. A call comes when equity falls to 25% of the position value: (1,000P - 25,000) / 1,000P = 0.25, so 750P = 25,000 and P = USD33.33.","stem":"An investor buys 1,000 shares at USD50 using an initial margin requirement of 50%. The maintenance margin requirement is 25%. A margin call will be triggered at a share price closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["market order, which guarantees execution at the best available price.","buy limit order at USD20.00.","buy stop order at USD20.00."],"distractors":["A market order guarantees execution, not price, which is the opposite of what the investor wants.","","A buy stop becomes active only once the price rises to the trigger, so it is used to buy into strength rather than to cap a purchase price."],"id":"eq-007","ref":"Order types","solution":"A limit order caps the price paid, at the cost of no guarantee of execution. That is exactly the trade-off the investor has chosen: price certainty over execution certainty, which matters most in a thin market where a market order could fill well away from the last price.","stem":"An investor wants to buy a thinly traded share but is unwilling to pay more than USD20.00. The most appropriate instruction is a:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["technical analysis based on past prices and volumes.","fundamental analysis of public financial statements.","trading on information not yet released to the public."],"distractors":["","Fundamental analysis is defeated by semi-strong efficiency, which is a stronger condition than the one given.","Trading on private information is defeated only by strong-form efficiency, the strongest condition of the three."],"id":"eq-008","ref":"Forms of market efficiency","solution":"Weak-form efficiency means past prices and volumes are already reflected in the current price, so studying them cannot produce abnormal returns. Fundamental analysis may still work unless the market is semi-strong efficient, and private information may still work unless it is strong-form efficient.","stem":"If a market is weak-form efficient, the analytical approach least likely to generate abnormal returns is:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["reduces the index's concentration in the largest companies.","reflects the shares actually available for investors to buy.","removes the need to rebalance the index."],"distractors":["Float adjustment often does reduce some weights, but that is a side effect rather than the purpose, and a large company with a full float keeps its weight.","","Rebalancing is still needed as prices move and as share counts and float change."],"id":"eq-009","ref":"Free float adjustment","solution":"Weighting by shares available to the market means index weights are achievable in practice. Counting closely held or government stakes would set weights that tracking funds cannot buy, producing a benchmark nobody can replicate.","stem":"Most widely used equity indices weight constituents by float-adjusted market capitalisation rather than total market capitalisation. The reason is most likely that float adjustment:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["a claim on dividends that accrues if unpaid, and no vote in most circumstances.","a claim ranking behind ordinary shares in liquidation, with voting rights attached.","a fixed maturity date on which the issuer must repay the par amount."],"distractors":["","Preference shares rank ahead of ordinary shares, not behind, and typically carry no vote.","Preference shares are usually perpetual. A fixed redemption date is the mark of a debt instrument, though some issues are redeemable."],"id":"eq-010","ref":"Preference shares","solution":"A cumulative preference share carries a dividend entitlement that accumulates when skipped and must be cleared before ordinary dividends resume. In exchange the holder normally gives up voting rights, and ranks ahead of ordinary shareholders but behind creditors in liquidation.","stem":"Compared with ordinary shares of the same issuer, cumulative preference shares are most likely to offer:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["higher returns on capital, because entry is difficult.","lower returns on capital, because fixed costs depress margins.","returns equal to their cost of capital, because competition eventually erodes any advantage."],"distractors":["","High fixed costs raise operating leverage, which magnifies both good and bad outcomes rather than reducing returns on average.","Competition drives returns to the cost of capital where entry is easy. Barriers are what interrupt that process."],"id":"eq-011","ref":"Barriers to entry","solution":"Barriers to entry protect incumbent economics by keeping new capacity out, so excess returns persist for longer than in a contestable industry. Fixed costs cut both ways, but combined with licensing they are a barrier rather than a burden.","stem":"An industry has high fixed costs, an entrenched distribution network and a regulatory licensing regime. All else equal, incumbents in this industry are most likely to sustain:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["greater sensitivity to the business cycle and a higher beta.","less sensitivity to the business cycle and a lower beta.","similar sensitivity, since both sell physical goods."],"distractors":["This has the two the wrong way round. Capital goods are the cyclical end of the market.","","Selling physical goods says nothing about whether the purchase can be deferred, which is what drives cyclicality."],"id":"eq-012","ref":"Cyclical and defensive industries","solution":"Demand for staples holds up through a downturn because the purchases are non-discretionary, while capital equipment orders are postponed. Lower earnings cyclicality generally shows up as a lower beta.","stem":"An analyst compares a household staples producer with a construction equipment maker. Relative to the equipment maker, the staples producer's earnings are most likely to show:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["is unaffected by differences in capital structure.","reflects the cost of the debt each company carries.","adjusts for differences in depreciation policy between the two."],"distractors":["","It does the opposite: it deliberately looks past the cost of debt so that the comparison is not distorted by it.","EBITDA excludes depreciation altogether rather than adjusting for differing policies, which is a related but separate advantage over earnings-based multiples."],"id":"eq-013","ref":"Enterprise value multiples","solution":"Enterprise value covers both debt and equity claims and EBITDA is struck before interest, so the multiple compares the whole business against the earnings available to all funders. A price to earnings ratio mixes an equity-only numerator with an after-interest denominator, which makes leveraged and unleveraged companies hard to compare.","stem":"An analyst compares two companies with very different levels of debt. Compared with the price to earnings ratio, an enterprise value to EBITDA multiple is most likely preferable because it:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["generates most of its value from a skilled workforce and brand.","holds mainly liquid, separately marketable assets and is expected to be wound up.","is growing rapidly and reinvesting all of its cash flow."],"distractors":["Intangibles like a workforce and brand rarely appear at anything like their economic value on the balance sheet, so this is the worst case for an asset-based approach.","","A growth company's value lies in future cash flows rather than its current asset base."],"id":"eq-014","ref":"Asset-based valuation","solution":"Adding up the market value of assets less liabilities makes sense where the assets can be valued individually and the going-concern premium is small or absent, such as a holding company being liquidated. It undervalues businesses whose worth lies in intangibles.","stem":"Asset-based valuation is most appropriate for a company that:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["rise by approximately the amount of the dividend.","fall by approximately the amount of the dividend.","be unchanged, because the dividend was announced earlier."],"distractors":["The holder loses entitlement to the payment, so the price falls rather than rises.","","The announcement is when the market learns of the dividend. The ex-date is when entitlement changes hands, and that is a separate price event."],"id":"eq-015","ref":"The ex-dividend date","solution":"A buyer on or after the ex-dividend date does not receive the declared dividend, so the share is worth less by roughly that amount. The cash is leaving the company, so the drop reflects value transferred to shareholders rather than value destroyed.","stem":"All else equal, on the ex-dividend date the price of a share is most likely to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["increase earnings per share.","decrease earnings per share.","leave earnings per share unchanged."],"distractors":["","This is the outcome where the funding cost exceeds the earnings yield, which is the reverse of the figures given.","Earnings per share is unchanged only where the after-tax funding cost exactly equals the earnings yield."],"id":"eq-016","ref":"Share repurchases and earnings per share","solution":"Earnings per share rises where the after-tax return given up on the funds used is below the earnings yield of the shares bought. Here the company forgoes 2.0% to retire shares yielding 6.0%, so the reduction in the share count outweighs the lost interest income.","stem":"A company funds a share repurchase entirely from cash currently earning 2.0% after tax. Its earnings yield is 6.0%. The repurchase will most likely:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["the underlying shares, with no currency exposure because the receipt trades in US dollars.","the underlying shares, including exposure to the yen against the US dollar.","a US dollar bond issued by the depositary bank."],"distractors":["Quoting in dollars does not remove currency risk. The underlying asset is still priced in yen.","","The depositary holds the shares on the investor's behalf. It is not issuing debt."],"id":"eq-017","ref":"Depositary receipts","solution":"A depositary receipt is a claim on the underlying foreign shares held by a depositary bank. The trading currency changes the settlement convenience, not the economics: the receipt's value still moves with both the share price in yen and the yen against the dollar.","stem":"A US investor buys a sponsored American depositary receipt over the shares of a Japanese company. The investor's exposure is most accurately described as:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["higher.","lower.","the same."],"distractors":["A larger denominator produces a smaller ratio, not a larger one.","","The two are equal only where earnings are expected to be flat."],"id":"eq-018","ref":"Trailing and forward multiples","solution":"The forward multiple divides the same price by a larger expected earnings figure, so it is lower. This is why comparing one company on a trailing basis with another on a forward basis is misleading.","stem":"A company's earnings are expected to grow strongly over the coming year. Compared with its trailing price to earnings ratio, its forward price to earnings ratio calculated at the same share price will most likely be:"},{"answer":1,"calc":true,"choices":["6.0%","8.5%","9.0%"],"distractors":["This is the price return alone, which omits the dividends.","","This compounds the two components rather than adding them, which overstates the total at these magnitudes."],"id":"eq-019","ref":"Price return and total return indices","solution":"The price return is (4,240 - 4,000) / 4,000 = 6.0%. Adding the dividend yield of 2.5% gives a total return of 8.5%. A price return index understates what an investor earned by exactly the income it ignores.","stem":"An index rises from 4,000 to 4,240 over a year, and constituents paid dividends equal to 2.5% of the opening index level. The total return on the index is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["dividend discount model using an assumed future payout.","free cash flow to equity model.","justified trailing price to earnings multiple."],"distractors":["Assuming a payout the company has never made makes the model's central input pure guesswork.","","The justified trailing multiple is itself derived from the dividend discount model, so it inherits the same problem."],"id":"eq-020","ref":"Free cash flow to equity","solution":"Free cash flow to equity measures the cash that could be paid out whether or not it is, so it works where there is no dividend history to project. A dividend discount model applied to an assumed payout rests the whole valuation on an assumption the company has never validated.","stem":"An analyst values a company that pays no dividend and is unlikely to start soon. The most appropriate of the following approaches is a:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["the maximum loss is unlimited, because there is no ceiling on the share price.","the maximum loss equals the proceeds of the sale.","dividends paid on the borrowed share accrue to the short seller."],"distractors":["","This describes the maximum gain on a short, which occurs if the share goes to zero.","The short seller owes the dividend to the lender of the share rather than receiving it."],"id":"eq-021","ref":"Short selling","solution":"A long position can lose no more than the amount invested, because the price stops at zero. A short position loses as the price rises, and there is no upper bound on how far it can rise, which is why short positions demand active risk control.","stem":"An investor sells a share short. The feature that most accurately distinguishes this position from a long position is that:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["more frequent rebalancing, and give greater weight to smaller companies.","less frequent rebalancing, and give greater weight to smaller companies.","more frequent rebalancing, and give greater weight to larger companies."],"distractors":["","Capitalisation weighting is the one that needs little rebalancing, because a rising price automatically raises the weight.","Equal weighting reduces the influence of the largest companies rather than increasing it."],"id":"eq-022","ref":"Equal weighting and rebalancing","solution":"Equal weights drift apart as prices move, so the index has to be reset periodically, whereas capitalisation weights self-adjust. Giving every constituent the same weight necessarily shifts weight away from the largest companies toward the smallest.","stem":"Compared with a market capitalisation weighted index of the same constituents, an equal weighted index will most likely require:"}],"ethics":[{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["acceptable, because the largest holders have the greatest economic interest in the change.","a violation of the Standard on fair dealing.","a violation of the Standard on preservation of confidentiality."],"distractors":["Fair dealing does not permit differential treatment by economic interest. It permits different levels of service that clients knowingly pay for, which is not the same thing.","","Confidentiality concerns information about clients, not the timing of the firm's own research distribution."],"id":"eth-001","ref":"Standard III(B) Fair Dealing","solution":"Fair dealing requires that a change in recommendation be communicated to all clients for whom it is suitable at substantially the same time. The size of a holding is not a permitted basis for giving some clients a head start. The correct approach is to distribute the change simultaneously and only then take orders.","stem":"An analyst finalises a downgrade of a widely held stock from buy to hold. Before the note is released through her firm's normal distribution channels, she telephones the three largest institutional clients of the firm to alert them to the change, reasoning that they hold the biggest positions and will suffer most from a delay. Her conduct is best described as:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["permissible, because the conclusion was assembled from public and non-material information.","a violation, because the supplier conversation was nonpublic.","a violation, because the conclusion itself is material and nonpublic until published."],"distractors":["","Nonpublic alone is not the test. Information must be both material and nonpublic before the prohibition bites, and a supplier's own volumes that do not identify the retailer are not material to the retailer.","An analyst's own conclusion is not inside information. If it were, no original research could ever be acted on."],"id":"eth-002","ref":"Standard II(A) Material Nonpublic Information","solution":"Assembling public information with pieces of nonpublic information that are individually non-material is the mosaic theory, and it is expressly permitted. The analyst's own conclusion is her work product; the value of original research is precisely that the market does not yet hold it.","stem":"An analyst builds an earnings estimate for a retailer by combining published industry footfall data, publicly filed accounts, her own store visits, and a conversation with a supplier who described its own shipment volumes without referring to the retailer. She reaches a conclusion materially different from consensus and publishes it. Her conduct is most likely:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["decline the visit, because accepting any travel from a covered company compromises independence.","accept the flight and accommodation, and disclose the arrangement to his employer and clients.","accept the flight and accommodation only if he first obtains written permission from the company."],"distractors":["A blanket refusal is stricter than the Standard requires and would prevent legitimate site work. The obligation is to prefer arm's length arrangements where they exist, and to disclose where they do not.","","Permission from the company being analysed is irrelevant. Disclosure runs to the member's employer and to clients, not to the subject of the research."],"id":"eth-003","ref":"Standard I(B) Independence and Objectivity","solution":"Members should use commercial transport at their own expense where it is available. Where it genuinely is not, accepting the issuer's transport and modest on-site accommodation is acceptable, provided the arrangement is disclosed so that readers can judge for themselves whether independence was affected.","stem":"A member covering a mining company is invited to tour a remote mine site. The company offers to fly him there on its corporate aircraft and accommodate him at the camp, as no commercial service reaches the site. To comply with the Standard on independence and objectivity, the member should most appropriately:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["local law, because members must comply with the law of the jurisdiction in which they work.","the stricter of the two, so the prohibition in the Standards applies.","either, provided the choice is disclosed to clients."],"distractors":["Complying with local law is necessary but not sufficient. Membership carries an obligation that local permissiveness cannot dilute.","","Disclosure does not create a licence. The stricter requirement applies whether or not anyone is told."],"id":"eth-004","ref":"Standard I(A) Knowledge of the Law","solution":"Where applicable law and the Code and Standards differ, members must comply with the stricter. Less strict local law does not lower the obligation; a stricter local law does raise it.","stem":"A member works in a country whose securities law permits trading on material nonpublic information. The Code and Standards prohibit it. The member's obligation is most accurately described as a duty to follow:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["report the colleague to the relevant regulator.","dissociate from the activity, which may mean removing his name from the work or resigning.","document his objection and continue, because responsibility now rests with compliance."],"distractors":["The Standards encourage this where local law demands it, but the requirement is dissociation. Making a regulatory report the test would misstate the obligation.","","Continuing to participate after raising the matter is still participation. Escalating internally does not discharge the duty on its own."],"id":"eth-005","ref":"Standard I(A) Knowledge of the Law","solution":"Members must not knowingly participate in a violation and must dissociate from it. Dissociation can mean refusing to sign off, asking to be removed from the assignment, or in the end leaving. Reporting to authorities is encouraged where the law requires it but is not itself the Standard's requirement.","stem":"A member becomes aware that a colleague is engaged in ongoing conduct that breaches the Standards. Having raised it with compliance without result, the member's minimum obligation is most appropriately to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["decline, because performance-linked benefits from clients are prohibited.","obtain written consent from all parties involved before accepting.","accept and record the arrangement in the client file."],"distractors":["There is no outright prohibition. The arrangement is permitted where it is consented to in writing by all parties.","","A note in the client file is not consent. The employer in particular must agree in writing."],"id":"eth-006","ref":"Standard I(B) Independence and Objectivity","solution":"An offer of additional compensation from a client that depends on future performance may create a conflict with the manager's duty to other clients. It is permitted only with written consent from all parties involved, which puts the employer and the affected clients on notice.","stem":"A portfolio manager's client offers her the use of a holiday villa for a fortnight if the portfolio beats its benchmark over the coming year. To comply with the Standards, the manager should most appropriately:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["disclose that the issuer paid for the research and describe the nature of the payment.","omit any recommendation, because issuer-paid research cannot support one.","carry no disclosure, because the fee was not contingent on the conclusion."],"distractors":["","A recommendation is permitted where there is a reasonable basis for it. The requirement is disclosure, not silence.","A flat fee removes the sharpest conflict, not the conflict. The commercial relationship still requires disclosure."],"id":"eth-007","ref":"Standard I(B) Independence and Objectivity","solution":"Issuer-paid research is permitted, but the relationship is exactly the sort of conflict a reader needs to know about. The fee arrangement must be disclosed, including that it is flat rather than contingent, so the reader can weigh the independence of the conclusion.","stem":"A research firm is paid a flat fee by a small issuer to write and distribute coverage of it. The firm is not paid any bonus tied to the conclusion reached. To comply with the Standards, the report must most appropriately:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["acceptable, because published research is in the public domain.","plagiarism, and a violation of the Standard on misrepresentation.","acceptable if she has independently verified the analysis."],"distractors":["Publication is not a waiver of authorship. The obligation is to attribute, not to avoid published sources.","","Verification does not cure the misattribution. It is a separate obligation to have a reasonable basis for the conclusion."],"id":"eth-008","ref":"Standard I(C) Misrepresentation","solution":"Using another party's work substantially as one's own, without attribution, is plagiarism and misrepresents the authorship of the analysis. Acknowledged excerpts with the source named are the remedy. Recognised statistical and economic series, and factual data from public bodies, may be used without attribution.","stem":"An analyst reproduces several paragraphs of another firm's published research in her own note, changing a few words, without naming the source. Her conduct is best described as:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["acceptable, because it is described as an expectation rather than a promise.","a misrepresentation, because it implies a guarantee the manager cannot give.","acceptable if the past record is accurately stated."],"distractors":["Softening the verb does not change what a reader takes from it. The test is the impression created, not the grammar.","","An accurate history does not license a forward assurance. The two claims are judged separately."],"id":"eth-009","ref":"Standard I(C) Misrepresentation","solution":"Members must not make any statement that misrepresents what an investment can be expected to deliver. Presenting an inherently uncertain equity return as something that will recur every year implies assurance the manager has no ability to provide, however it is worded.","stem":"A marketing document for an equity fund states that the strategy has never had a losing year and that the manager expects to continue delivering positive returns every year. The statement about future returns is most likely:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["trade, because he obtained the information passively and owes no duty to the company.","refrain from trading and from passing the information on.","trade only after publishing a note describing what he heard, so that the market is informed."],"distractors":["Passive receipt is not a defence. Nothing in the Standard turns on whether the member sought the information out.","","A member cannot make information public on the issuer's behalf. Publishing overheard confidential information would compound the problem rather than cure it."],"id":"eth-010","ref":"Standard II(A) Material Nonpublic Information","solution":"The prohibition attaches to the information, not to how it was acquired. Overheard information about an unannounced takeover is both material and nonpublic, so the member must neither act on it nor pass it on until the market has it.","stem":"On a flight, a member overhears two executives of a listed company discussing an unannounced takeover of a rival. He has no relationship with either company. His most appropriate course of action is to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["a firewall separating the two units, supported by a restricted list.","a requirement that all employees pre-clear personal trades.","a policy of disclosing the conflict in research notes on the target."],"distractors":["","Pre-clearance addresses employees' own accounts. It does nothing about the firm trading client money on the back of leaked deal information.","Disclosure cannot cure this. Confidential deal information must be contained, and a note that hinted at it would itself be a breach."],"id":"eth-011","ref":"Standard II(A) Material Nonpublic Information","solution":"An information barrier between the unit holding the confidential information and the unit trading is the primary control, with a restricted or watch list operating alongside it to catch the affected securities. The point is to prevent the information reaching the traders at all.","stem":"An investment bank's advisory team is working on an unannounced acquisition while its asset management arm continues to trade the target's shares. The control most directly aimed at this risk is best described as:"},{"answer":2,"calc":false,"choices":["permissible, because the trades were executed at market prices and no false statement was made.","information-based manipulation.","transaction-based manipulation, and a violation of the Standard on market manipulation."],"distractors":["Executing at market prices does not help. The deception lies in manufacturing apparent liquidity that does not exist.","Information-based manipulation involves spreading false or misleading information. Here the misleading signal is created by the trades themselves.",""],"id":"eth-012","ref":"Standard II(B) Market Manipulation","solution":"Trades executed with the purpose of creating a misleading impression of activity are transaction-based manipulation. No misstatement is needed: the deception is carried by the volume figures the market sees.","stem":"A trader arranges a series of matched buy and sell orders between two accounts he controls in a thinly traded stock, so that reported volume rises sharply and the stock screens as liquid to prospective buyers. His conduct is best described as:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["the company, because it appointed her and pays the mandate.","the beneficiaries of the scheme.","the finance director, as the authorised representative of the client."],"distractors":["The sponsor signs the mandate but is not the client for the purpose of this duty. Confusing the two is the trap the Standard exists to prevent.","","An individual's authority to instruct does not transfer the duty. The beneficiaries' interests still come first."],"id":"eth-013","ref":"Standard III(A) Loyalty, Prudence and Care","solution":"Where a member manages a pension or trust, the client is the beneficiaries rather than the entity that hired her. Their interests govern, so a request that serves the sponsor's commercial aims rather than the beneficiaries' must be declined.","stem":"A member manages the assets of a company pension scheme. The company's finance director asks her to shift the portfolio toward the shares of the company's own suppliers, to strengthen those commercial relationships. Her duty of loyalty in this situation runs most directly to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["the research benefits the clients whose brokerage paid for it.","the arrangement is approved in advance by the broker.","the additional cost is below a threshold set by the manager."],"distractors":["","The broker's approval is irrelevant. The question is whose money is being spent and who benefits.","A self-set threshold does not make the spending legitimate. The test is benefit to the client, not size."],"id":"eth-014","ref":"Standard III(A) Loyalty, Prudence and Care","solution":"Client brokerage belongs to the client, so it may be used for research only where that research benefits the clients whose trades generated it, and best execution is still owed. Using client commissions to buy something the firm consumes for its own purposes is a breach.","stem":"A manager directs client trades to a broker that charges more than the cheapest available execution, in exchange for research the manager uses across all portfolios. This arrangement is most likely acceptable where:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["to the accounts that indicated interest first.","pro rata across all suitable accounts that indicated interest.","to the accounts paying the highest fees, disclosed in advance."],"distractors":["First come, first served rewards whoever happened to be contacted earliest, which usually reflects the firm's own calling order rather than anything about the client.","","Fee level is not a permitted basis for allocating a scarce security, and disclosing an unfair practice does not make it fair."],"id":"eth-015","ref":"Standard III(B) Fair Dealing","solution":"A pro rata allocation across suitable accounts on the basis of indicated interest treats clients fairly where supply is short. Allocating by order of arrival or by fee level advantages some clients on grounds unrelated to suitability.","stem":"A firm receives an allocation in an oversubscribed new issue that is smaller than the total indications of interest from suitable client accounts. The most appropriate way to allocate it is:"},{"answer":2,"calc":false,"choices":["execute the trade, because an unsolicited instruction from the client overrides the policy statement.","refuse, because the trade is inconsistent with the investment policy statement.","discuss the conflict with the client and, if the trade proceeds, consider whether the policy statement needs updating."],"distractors":["Simply executing ignores the adviser's own suitability obligation, which does not disappear because the client asked.","A flat refusal is not required either. The Standard contemplates the client being informed and the documentation being brought into line.",""],"id":"eth-016","ref":"Standard III(C) Suitability","solution":"An unsolicited trade outside the policy calls for a conversation about how it fits the client's objectives. Where the trade is small enough not to disturb the portfolio's overall suitability it may proceed; where it would, the policy statement itself has to be revisited in writing.","stem":"A client with a conservative investment policy statement telephones his adviser and instructs him to buy a speculative single stock, acknowledging that it sits outside the agreed policy. The adviser's most appropriate response is to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["acceptable, because she acted on a reasonable basis in what she judged to be investors' interests.","a violation, because a pooled fund must be managed to its stated mandate rather than to the manager's view.","acceptable, provided the change is disclosed in the next quarterly report."],"distractors":["A well-founded view does not authorise a departure from the mandate. Investors chose the strategy, not the manager's discretion over asset classes.","","Reporting a breach after the fact does not authorise it. The mandate had to change first."],"id":"eth-017","ref":"Standard III(C) Suitability","solution":"Where a member manages a pooled vehicle to a stated strategy, suitability is judged against that mandate rather than against individual investors' circumstances. Investors bought the stated exposure, and departing from it deprives them of what they selected.","stem":"A manager runs a fund with a stated mandate to track a broad emerging market index. She becomes convinced that developed market equities offer better value and shifts a quarter of the fund into them. Her conduct is best described as:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["acceptable, because closed accounts are no longer managed by the firm.","misleading, because excluding terminated accounts overstates the record.","acceptable if the number of excluded accounts is disclosed."],"distractors":["Whether the firm still manages the money has no bearing on whether it managed it during the period being shown.","","Counting the omissions does not repair the figures. The excluded accounts belong in the return itself."],"id":"eth-018","ref":"Standard III(D) Performance Presentation","solution":"Removing terminated accounts introduces survivorship bias and flatters the record. A composite must include every account that ran the strategy over the period, including those since closed.","stem":"A firm presents a composite record for its equity strategy that includes only accounts still open at the presentation date. Accounts closed after poor results were removed. The presentation is most likely:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["state clearly that the results are simulated and not achieved with client money.","avoid all reference to the historical results.","present the results net of an estimated fee, which makes the basis clear."],"distractors":["","There is no prohibition on showing model results. The requirement is that their nature is disclosed.","Deducting a fee does not tell the reader that no client money was at risk, which is the material point."],"id":"eth-019","ref":"Standard III(D) Performance Presentation","solution":"Back-tested or model results may be shown, but the reader must be told plainly what they are. Presenting them in a way that could be read as an actual track record misrepresents performance.","stem":"A member markets a new quantitative strategy using returns produced by applying the model to historical data, as no live track record yet exists. To comply with the Standards, the presentation must most appropriately:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["ended, because the client relationship has ended.","continuing, so the member should not discuss the former client's affairs.","continuing, unless the former client's information is already known in the market."],"distractors":["Termination of the mandate does not release the member. Treating it as an expiry date is the common error.","","Market rumour does not discharge the duty, and a member confirming it would be the source of the disclosure."],"id":"eth-020","ref":"Standard III(E) Preservation of Confidentiality","solution":"The duty of confidentiality survives the end of the relationship. Information about a former client's holdings and strategy stays confidential unless disclosure is required by law, concerns illegal activities, or the client permits it.","stem":"A former client's account was closed two years ago. A prospective client asks the member about the type of strategy that former client ran, naming him. The member's obligation of confidentiality is most accurately described as:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["acceptable, because preparations to compete may be made before resignation.","a violation, because he used his employer's property and solicited its clients while still employed.","acceptable, because clients are free to choose their own adviser."],"distractors":["Preparation is permitted; misappropriation and solicitation are not. The distinction is what the member takes and when he approaches clients.","","Clients are free to move, but that freedom does not entitle the member to use his employer's records or to canvass them on the employer's time."],"id":"eth-021","ref":"Standard IV(A) Loyalty","solution":"A member may take steps to prepare to compete, such as registering a company or arranging premises, so long as those steps do not breach the duty of loyalty. Taking the employer's client records and soliciting its clients while still employed crosses that line, because both the list and the client relationships belong to the employer.","stem":"A member intends to resign and set up a competing firm. While still employed, and without his employer's knowledge, he copies the client contact list and begins soliciting those clients. His conduct is best described as:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["obtain written consent from all parties involved, including his employer.","disclose the arrangement to his clients only.","confirm that the fee is below his employer's materiality threshold."],"distractors":["","Clients are not the only party with an interest. The employer's consent is the requirement here.","Size is not the test. Consent is required whether the fee is large or small."],"id":"eth-022","ref":"Standard IV(B) Additional Compensation Arrangements","solution":"An arrangement that may create a conflict with the employer's interests, or compete with it, requires written consent from all parties involved before it is accepted. Verbal notice or an informal mention does not meet the Standard.","stem":"A member is offered a seat on the board of a private company, with a fee payable to him personally, in return for advice that overlaps with his day job. Before accepting, he must most appropriately:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["excused, because the task was delegated to a competent person.","still responsible, because delegation does not discharge supervisory responsibility.","excused, provided the delegation was documented in advance."],"distractors":["Competence of the delegate is a requirement, not a defence. The supervisor still owes a duty to see the system functions.","","Documentation helps establish that a system exists. It does not remove the obligation to see that the system detects what it is meant to detect."],"id":"eth-023","ref":"Standard IV(C) Responsibilities of Supervisors","solution":"A supervisor may delegate tasks but remains responsible for ensuring that those to whom work is delegated have the necessary competence and that adequate procedures are in place and working. Delegation transfers the work, not the responsibility.","stem":"A supervisor delegates monitoring of trade allocations to a competent junior colleague, who fails to detect a pattern of favourable allocation to one account. The supervisor's position is most accurately described as:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["acceptable, because the model was supplied by a reputable provider.","a violation, because he has not established a reasonable and adequate basis for the recommendation.","acceptable if he discloses that the analysis came from a third party."],"distractors":["A provider's reputation is a starting point for due diligence, not a substitute for it.","","Disclosure tells the reader where the analysis came from. It does not supply the diligence the member owed."],"id":"eth-024","ref":"Standard V(A) Diligence and Reasonable Basis","solution":"Relying on outside research is permitted, but the member must make reasonable enquiries into the soundness of what he relies on. Recommending a product on the basis of a model whose assumptions he has never looked at leaves him without a reasonable basis.","stem":"A member recommends a structured product to clients on the strength of a third-party model whose assumptions and inputs he has not examined. His conduct is best described as:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["present the approval as a fact, since the recommendation depends on it.","identify the approval as an assumption and describe the risk that it is refused.","omit the assumption, because forecasts are inherently uncertain."],"distractors":["Stating a contested forecast as a fact is the misrepresentation the Standard is aimed at.","","Uncertainty is a reason to disclose the assumption, not to hide it."],"id":"eth-025","ref":"Standard V(B) Communication with Clients","solution":"Members must distinguish fact from opinion and identify the important factors and risks on which a recommendation rests. An assumption that carries the whole conclusion is exactly what the reader needs flagged as an assumption.","stem":"A note recommending a stock rests on the analyst's forecast that a contested regulatory approval will be granted. To comply with the Standard on communication with clients, the note should most appropriately:"},{"answer":2,"calc":false,"choices":["three years.","five years.","seven years."],"distractors":["Shorter than the recommendation, and shorter than most regulatory regimes require.","A common statutory period in some jurisdictions, but not the recommendation in the Standards.",""],"id":"eth-026","ref":"Standard V(C) Record Retention","solution":"Where no regulator or firm policy imposes a longer period, CFA Institute recommends retaining supporting records for at least seven years. Records developed in the course of employment belong to the employer.","stem":"In the absence of any local regulatory requirement, the minimum period for which a member should retain the records supporting research and investment recommendations is most likely:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["sell the position before publishing, because the conflict cannot otherwise be managed.","disclose the holding prominently in the report.","publish without disclosure, because the recommendation rests on the analysis rather than the holding."],"distractors":["Divestment is not required by the Standard, and a firm may impose its own stricter policy. The obligation in the Standards is to disclose.","","Whether the analysis is sound is a separate question. The reader is entitled to know about the interest and judge for themselves."],"id":"eth-027","ref":"Standard VI(A) Disclosure of Conflicts","solution":"Members must disclose all matters that could reasonably be expected to impair their objectivity, and a personal stake in a covered company is the clearest example. The disclosure must be prominent and in plain language, so a reader can weigh it.","stem":"An analyst holds a substantial personal position in a company she is about to initiate coverage on with a buy recommendation. Her most appropriate course of action is to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["trade his own account alongside the clients, so that all participants receive the same average price.","complete the client orders before trading for his own account.","trade his own account first, provided the size is immaterial relative to the client orders."],"distractors":["Sharing an average price still gives the personal account a share of a scarce opportunity that belonged to clients first.","","Immateriality to the manager is not the test. Priority runs to the client regardless of the personal order's size."],"id":"eth-028","ref":"Standard VI(B) Priority of Transactions","solution":"Client transactions take precedence over transactions for the member's own benefit. In a small company where the buying itself moves the price, trading personally alongside or ahead of clients takes part of the opportunity the clients paid for.","stem":"A manager decides to build a position in a small company across several client portfolios over the coming week, and also wants the stock for his own account. To comply with the Standards he should most appropriately:"},{"answer":2,"calc":false,"choices":["her employer only.","the referred client only.","her employer and the client, before the client engages the tax adviser."],"distractors":["The employer needs to know, but so does the person whose choice of adviser is being steered.","The client needs to know, but the employer's interest in referral arrangements is also engaged.",""],"id":"eth-029","ref":"Standard VI(C) Referral Fees","solution":"Consideration received for a referral must be disclosed to the employer and to the client or prospective client, and the disclosure must come before the engagement so the client can assess whether the recommendation was influenced by the payment.","stem":"A member receives a fee from a tax adviser each time she introduces a client to him. To comply with the Standards she must most appropriately disclose the arrangement to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["CFA Level III candidate.","CFA Level II, having passed all exams to date.","Chartered Financial Analyst, Level III."],"distractors":["","There is no such thing as CFA Level II as a credential. Using the marks to describe progress implies a designation that does not exist.","The designation belongs only to charterholders in good standing, and it is never qualified by a level."],"id":"eth-030","ref":"Standard VII(B) Reference to the CFA Designation","solution":"A candidate may state that she is a candidate for the level she is currently enrolled in, in a factual sentence or as a candidate designation. There is no partial designation, so the marks cannot be used to describe a level passed.","stem":"A candidate has passed the Level II exam and is enrolled for Level III. The most appropriate way for her to describe her status on a business card is:"}],"fi":[{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["-1.80%","-0.29%","+1.80%"],"distractors":["","This divides rather than multiplies, 0.0025 / 7.20 x 100.","This has the sign the wrong way round. A rise in yield lowers price."],"id":"fi-001","ref":"Modified duration and price change","solution":"The first order approximation is minus modified duration multiplied by the change in yield: -7.20 x 0.0025 = -0.0180, a fall of 1.80%. Ignoring convexity slightly overstates the loss, because the price yield curve is convex.","stem":"A bond has a modified duration of 7.20. Its yield to maturity rises by 25 basis points. Ignoring convexity, the percentage change in the bond's price is closest to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["6.25","6.50","6.76"],"distractors":["","This is the Macaulay duration itself, undiscounted.","This multiplies by 1.04 instead of dividing, which moves in the wrong direction."],"id":"fi-002","ref":"Macaulay and modified duration","solution":"Modified duration is Macaulay duration divided by one plus the yield per period: 6.50 / 1.04 = 6.25. Modified duration is always the smaller of the two for a positive yield.","stem":"An annual-pay bond has a Macaulay duration of 6.50 years and a yield to maturity of 4.0%. Its modified duration is closest to:"},{"answer":2,"calc":true,"choices":["3.99%","4.51%","5.01%"],"distractors":["This is roughly the average of the two spot rates and reflects no compounding relationship.","This interpolates between the spot rates rather than solving the no arbitrage condition. The forward must exceed the longer spot on an upward sloping curve.",""],"id":"fi-003","ref":"Implied forward rate","solution":"No arbitrage requires that investing for two years at the two-year spot equals investing for one year and rolling at the forward rate. So (1.04)^2 / 1.03 - 1 = 1.0816 / 1.03 - 1 = 5.01%. The forward rate sits above the two-year spot because the spot curve is upward sloping.","stem":"The one-year spot rate is 3.00% and the two-year spot rate is 4.00%, both annually compounded. The one-year rate implied one year forward is closest to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["97.33","100.00","102.75"],"distractors":["","A bond prices at par only when its coupon equals its yield.","This is the premium the bond would trade at if the yield were below the coupon, which is the wrong direction."],"id":"fi-004","ref":"Pricing a bond off its yield","solution":"Discount each cash flow at 6.0%: 5 / 1.06 + 5 / 1.06^2 + 105 / 1.06^3 = 4.717 + 4.450 + 88.160 = 97.33. The bond trades at a discount because the coupon is below the required yield.","stem":"A bond with three years to maturity pays an annual coupon of 5.0% on a face value of 100 and is priced to yield 6.0%. Its price is closest to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["the coupon rate is below the yield to maturity.","the coupon rate is above the yield to maturity.","the coupon rate equals the yield to maturity."],"distractors":["","A coupon above the required yield produces a premium price, not a discount.","Equality produces a par price."],"id":"fi-005","ref":"Coupon, yield and price","solution":"A bond trades at a discount when the coupon it pays is less than the return the market demands, so the price falls until the total return to maturity, coupon plus the pull to par, matches the yield.","stem":"A bond is quoted at a price below its par value. The relationship between its coupon rate and its yield to maturity is most accurately described as:"},{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["5.91%","6.00%","6.09%"],"distractors":["","This treats the two conventions as interchangeable, which they are not.","This compounds in the wrong direction, treating 6% as the semiannual bond basis figure and converting it to an annual effective rate."],"id":"fi-006","ref":"Semiannual bond basis","solution":"Convert by finding the semiannual rate that compounds to the annual figure and doubling it: 2 x [(1.06)^0.5 - 1] = 2 x 0.02956 = 5.91%. A more frequent compounding convention needs a lower stated rate to reach the same effective return.","stem":"A bond's yield to maturity is quoted as 6.00% on an annual-pay basis. Stated on a semiannual bond basis, the yield is closest to:"},{"answer":2,"calc":true,"choices":["-7.30%","-7.00%","-6.70%"],"distractors":["This subtracts the convexity adjustment. For a positively convex bond the adjustment is added whichever way yields move.","This is the duration-only estimate, before any convexity adjustment.",""],"id":"fi-007","ref":"Convexity adjustment","solution":"The duration term is -7.0 x 0.01 = -7.00%. The convexity term is 0.5 x 60 x (0.01)^2 = +0.30%. Together the estimate is -6.70%. Convexity always works in the holder's favour for a bond without embedded options, cushioning losses and amplifying gains.","stem":"A bond has a modified duration of 7.0 and an annual convexity of 60. If its yield rises by 100 basis points, the estimated percentage change in price, including the convexity adjustment, is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":true,"choices":["USD588","USD5,880","USD58,800"],"distractors":["This is the effect of a tenth of a basis point, or the money duration scaled by 0.00001.","","This is the money duration times 0.001, which is the effect of ten basis points rather than one."],"id":"fi-008","ref":"Price value of a basis point","solution":"Money duration is modified duration multiplied by the full price: 6.0 x 9,800,000 = USD58.8m. The price value of a basis point is that figure times 0.0001, so USD5,880 per basis point.","stem":"A portfolio holds a bond position with a full market value of USD9,800,000 and a modified duration of 6.0. The price value of a basis point for the position is closest to:"},{"answer":2,"calc":true,"choices":["3.50%","4.00%","4.02%"],"distractors":["This interpolates between the one-year spot and the par rate rather than solving for the spot.","This is the par rate, which equals the spot rate only for a single-cash-flow instrument.",""],"id":"fi-009","ref":"Bootstrapping a spot rate","solution":"Price the par bond off spot rates: 100 = 4 / 1.03 + 104 / (1 + z2)^2. The first term is 3.883, so 104 / (1 + z2)^2 = 96.117, giving (1 + z2)^2 = 1.0820 and z2 = 4.02%. The two-year spot sits slightly above the par rate because the first coupon is discounted at the lower one-year rate.","stem":"The one-year spot rate is 3.00%. A two-year bond paying an annual coupon of 4.00% trades at par. The two-year spot rate is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["rising more, because the embedded option gains value for the holder.","rising less, because the call caps the price the holder can realise.","identical, because the call is exercisable only at the issuer's discretion."],"distractors":["The option is the issuer's, not the holder's, so the holder is short it and is compensated with a higher yield.","","Issuer discretion is exactly why the holder is disadvantaged. The issuer calls when it suits the issuer."],"id":"fi-010","ref":"Callable bonds and negative convexity","solution":"The call belongs to the issuer, so as yields fall the likelihood of redemption rises and the price flattens toward the call price. That flattening is negative convexity: the bond gains less on a rally than it loses on a selloff.","stem":"As market yields fall well below the coupon on a callable bond, the price behaviour of the bond compared with an otherwise identical non-callable bond is best described as:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["plus the value of the put option.","minus the value of the put option.","unchanged, because the put affects yield rather than price."],"distractors":["","Subtracting the option value is the callable case, where the issuer owns the option.","Price and yield are two ways of saying the same thing. An option that benefits the holder shows up in both."],"id":"fi-011","ref":"Putable bonds","solution":"A put lets the holder sell the bond back to the issuer, usually at par, which is valuable when yields rise. The holder owns that option, so the putable bond is worth more than the straight bond and carries a lower yield.","stem":"The value of a putable bond, relative to an otherwise identical bond without the put, is most accurately described as the value of the straight bond:"},{"answer":2,"calc":false,"choices":["modified duration, calculated from the bond's yield to maturity.","Macaulay duration, weighted by the timing of the promised cash flows.","effective duration, estimated by repricing the bond under shifted benchmark curves."],"distractors":["Modified duration assumes the cash flows are fixed, which is precisely what a call breaks.","Macaulay duration has the same problem, and it is a weighted average time rather than a sensitivity measure.",""],"id":"fi-012","ref":"Effective duration","solution":"Where cash flows themselves change with the level of rates, a measure built on promised cash flows misstates the risk. Effective duration reprices the bond under a small parallel shift in the benchmark curve, so it captures the option's response.","stem":"For a bond with an embedded call option, the most appropriate measure of interest rate sensitivity is:"},{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["USD80,000","USD200,000","USD400,000"],"distractors":["","This applies the probability of default to the full exposure and ignores recovery.","This applies loss given default to the exposure without weighting by the probability of default."],"id":"fi-013","ref":"Expected loss","solution":"Expected loss is the probability of default multiplied by loss given default multiplied by the exposure: 0.02 x 0.40 x 10,000,000 = USD80,000. Recovery of 60% is what separates expected loss from the full exposure at risk.","stem":"An analyst estimates a 2.0% probability of default over the next year on a USD10m exposure, with an expected loss given default of 40%. The expected credit loss for the year is closest to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["senior secured, senior unsecured, subordinated.","senior unsecured, senior secured, subordinated.","subordinated, senior secured, senior unsecured."],"distractors":["","Collateral is what puts secured above unsecured, so this reverses the top two.","Subordinated debt ranks last, not first. Its higher yield is compensation for exactly that."],"id":"fi-014","ref":"Seniority and recovery","solution":"Secured claims rank first because they have recourse to specific collateral, then senior unsecured, then subordinated debt, which is paid only once the more senior classes are satisfied. Recovery rates in practice follow that order.","stem":"Ranking the following claims on the same issuer from highest to lowest expected recovery in a default, the correct order is most likely:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["BB+ or Ba1.","BBB- or Baa3.","A- or A3."],"distractors":["This is the highest high yield rating, one notch below the boundary.","","This sits comfortably inside investment grade, several notches above the boundary."],"id":"fi-015","ref":"Investment grade boundary","solution":"BBB- from Standard and Poor's and Baa3 from Moody's are the lowest investment grade ratings. One notch below, at BB+ or Ba1, the issuer is high yield, and that boundary matters because many mandates prohibit holdings below it.","stem":"The lowest rating that is still considered investment grade is most likely:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["much lower, because the coupon resets to the prevailing rate.","about the same, because both instruments have the same maturity.","higher, because the coupon is uncertain."],"distractors":["","Maturity drives duration only where the cash flows are fixed. A reset breaks that link.","Uncertainty in the coupon is what reduces price sensitivity here, because the coupon moves with the market instead of the price."],"id":"fi-016","ref":"Floating rate notes","solution":"Because the coupon resets each quarter, the note's price stays near par and its interest rate duration is roughly the time to the next reset rather than the time to maturity. What remains sensitive is the credit spread, since that is fixed for the life of the note.","stem":"A floating rate note pays a quarterly coupon of three-month reference rate plus a fixed spread. Compared with a fixed-coupon bond of the same maturity, its price sensitivity to a parallel shift in the reference rate curve is most likely:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["a longer Macaulay duration, because principal is returned throughout.","a shorter Macaulay duration, because principal is returned throughout.","the same Macaulay duration, because the final maturity is identical."],"distractors":["Returning cash earlier shortens the weighted average time, not lengthens it.","","Final maturity is only one input. The timing of every cash flow matters, which is the point of a duration measure."],"id":"fi-017","ref":"Amortising and bullet structures","solution":"Macaulay duration is the weighted average time to receipt of cash flows. Returning principal along the way pulls that average forward, so the amortising bond is less exposed to a change in yields even though it matures on the same day.","stem":"Two bonds have the same final maturity, coupon and yield, but one repays principal in equal instalments over its life and the other repays it all at maturity. Compared with the bullet, the amortising bond will most likely have:"},{"answer":1,"calc":true,"choices":["15.00","15.45","45.00"],"distractors":["This applies the coupon to the unindexed par value, which is what a conventional bond would pay.","","This applies the inflation rate as though it were the coupon rate."],"id":"fi-018","ref":"Inflation-linked bonds","solution":"The principal is indexed first, to 1,000 x 1.03 = 1,030, and the real coupon rate is applied to the indexed principal: 1,030 x 1.5% = 15.45. Indexing the principal is what preserves the real value of both the coupon and the redemption.","stem":"An inflation-linked bond has a par value of 1,000, indexed to a price index, and pays a real coupon of 1.5% a year. Over the first year the index rises 3.0%. The coupon paid at the end of that year is closest to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["the collateral is of high credit quality and in demand.","the term of the agreement is longer.","the borrower has a weaker credit rating than the lender."],"distractors":["","A longer term generally raises the rate, since the lender is committed for longer.","A weaker borrower pays more, not less. Repo does reduce the lender's reliance on the borrower's standing, which is why the collateral is what drives the rate."],"id":"fi-019","ref":"Repurchase agreements","solution":"Repo is secured borrowing, so the rate reflects the quality and demand for the collateral as much as the borrower's own standing. High quality collateral that lenders want tightens the rate, and specific securities in short supply can trade at very low rates.","stem":"A dealer finances a bond position through a repurchase agreement. The repo rate charged will most likely be lower where:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["above the corresponding spot rates.","below the corresponding spot rates.","equal to the corresponding spot rates."],"distractors":["","This is the downward sloping case, where forwards sit below spots.","Equality holds only where the curve is flat."],"id":"fi-020","ref":"Yield curve shape and forward rates","solution":"A spot rate is an average of the forward rates covering the same period. For that average to be rising with maturity, each successive forward must sit above it, in the same way that a rising average requires each new observation to exceed it.","stem":"The spot curve is upward sloping at every maturity. The relationship between implied forward rates and spot rates of the same starting date is most accurately described as forward rates being:"},{"answer":1,"calc":true,"choices":["96.00","99.00","99.01"],"distractors":["This applies the 4.00% for a full year rather than for the 90 days the bill runs.","","This discounts the face value as though 4.00% were an add-on yield, 100 / (1 + 0.04 x 90/360)."],"id":"fi-021","ref":"Money market yield conventions","solution":"On a bank discount basis the discount is taken from face value: 100 x 0.04 x 90/360 = 1.00, so the price is 99.00. The discount rate understates the true return, because the interest is earned on 99.00 rather than on the 100 the rate is applied to.","stem":"A 90-day Treasury bill with a face value of 100 is quoted at a discount rate of 4.00% on a 360-day basis. Its purchase price is closest to:"},{"answer":2,"calc":false,"choices":["the coupon payments themselves.","the redemption of par at maturity.","the income earned on reinvested coupons."],"distractors":["The coupon amounts are fixed by the terms of the bond.","Par at maturity is also contractual, and the pull to par from a discount price is arithmetic.",""],"id":"fi-022","ref":"Sources of return on a bond","solution":"The coupons and the redemption amount are contractual, so absent default they are known. What is not known is the rate at which each coupon can be reinvested, and that reinvestment income is why a realised return can differ from the yield to maturity quoted at purchase.","stem":"An investor buys a coupon bond at a discount and holds it to maturity, reinvesting each coupon. The source of return that is not known with certainty at purchase is most likely:"}],"fsa":[{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["higher net income and higher ending inventory.","lower net income and lower ending inventory.","lower net income and higher ending inventory."],"distractors":["This is the FIFO outcome in a rising-price environment, not the LIFO one.","","Net income and inventory move in the same direction here, because the same cost either sits in cost of goods sold or stays on the balance sheet. They cannot both be affected favourably."],"id":"fs-001","ref":"LIFO and FIFO in rising prices","solution":"LIFO charges the most recently purchased, and therefore most expensive, units to cost of goods sold. Higher cost of goods sold means lower gross profit and lower net income, and what remains on the balance sheet is the older, cheaper layers, so ending inventory is lower.","stem":"Two otherwise identical companies hold the same physical inventory. Prices of that inventory have risen steadily through the year, and quantities have been stable. Compared with the company using FIFO, the company using LIFO will most likely report:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["lower cash flow from operations in the year of the expenditure.","higher cash flow from operations in the year of the expenditure.","higher total cash flow in the year of the expenditure."],"distractors":["This is the effect of expensing, which keeps the outflow inside operating activities.","","Total cash flow does not change. Only the section of the cash flow statement the outflow appears in changes."],"id":"fs-002","ref":"Capitalising against expensing","solution":"The cash paid is the same either way, but classification differs. Capitalising records the payment as an investing outflow, so operating cash flow is higher. Expensing records it within operating activities, so operating cash flow is lower. Total cash flow is identical under both treatments.","stem":"A company incurs an expenditure that it may either capitalise or expense as incurred. Compared with expensing it, capitalising the expenditure will most likely result in:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["revenue in December, because the cash has been received.","unearned revenue, a liability, until the service is delivered.","a prepaid expense, an asset, until the service is delivered."],"distractors":["This is cash basis accounting. Under accrual accounting the receipt of cash is neither necessary nor sufficient for revenue recognition.","","A prepaid expense arises when the company itself pays in advance for something it will receive. Here the company is the one that owes performance."],"id":"fs-003","ref":"Accrual accounting and unearned revenue","solution":"Revenue is recognised when the performance obligation is satisfied, not when cash arrives. Cash received in advance creates an obligation to deliver, so it is recorded as a liability and released to revenue in March as the service is performed.","stem":"In December a company receives cash from a customer for a service it will deliver the following March. Under accrual accounting, the December receipt is most appropriately recorded as:"},{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["USD45,000","USD50,000","USD55,000"],"distractors":["","This depreciates the full cost, ignoring the residual value that will not be consumed.","This adds the residual value to cost rather than deducting it."],"id":"fs-004","ref":"Straight-line depreciation","solution":"Straight-line depreciation spreads cost less residual value over the useful life: (500,000 - 50,000) / 10 = USD45,000 a year.","stem":"A company buys equipment for USD500,000, expects to use it for ten years, and estimates a residual value of USD50,000. Annual depreciation under the straight-line method is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["higher net income and higher total assets.","lower net income and lower total assets.","lower net income and higher total assets."],"distractors":["This is the later-life effect of an accelerated method, once the larger early charges have been taken.","","The same depreciation charge cannot both reduce income and increase the asset's carrying amount. They move together."],"id":"fs-005","ref":"Accelerated depreciation","solution":"An accelerated method charges more depreciation early, so the first year carries a larger expense and lower net income, and accumulated depreciation is larger so the carrying amount of the asset, and total assets, is lower. The pattern reverses in later years.","stem":"A company switches from straight-line to a double declining balance method for a newly acquired asset. Compared with straight line, in the first year of the asset's life it will most likely report:"},{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["0.71","1.43","1.50"],"distractors":["","This includes inventory but not prepayments, which is neither the quick ratio nor the current ratio.","This is the current ratio, 210 / 140, which counts every current asset."],"id":"fs-006","ref":"Quick ratio","solution":"The quick ratio counts only the most liquid current assets, so cash and receivables but not inventory or prepayments: (40 + 60) / 140 = 0.71.","stem":"A company reports cash of USD40m, receivables of USD60m, inventory of USD100m, prepaid expenses of USD10m and current liabilities of USD140m. Its quick ratio is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":true,"choices":["46 days","61 days","125 days"],"distractors":["This uses revenue rather than cost of goods sold in the turnover, which overstates turnover because revenue carries a margin.","","This divides average inventory by cost of goods sold and multiplies by a year of 12 rather than 365, or otherwise mixes the units."],"id":"fs-007","ref":"Days of inventory on hand","solution":"Inventory turnover is cost of goods sold divided by average inventory: 900 / 150 = 6.0 times. Days of inventory on hand is 365 / 6.0 = 61 days.","stem":"A company reports revenue of USD1,200m, cost of goods sold of USD900m and average inventory of USD150m. Its days of inventory on hand is closest to:"},{"answer":2,"calc":true,"choices":["10.0%","16.0%","20.0%"],"distractors":["This is return on assets, margin times turnover, before the leverage multiplier.","This multiplies the margin by leverage alone and omits asset turnover.",""],"id":"fs-008","ref":"DuPont decomposition","solution":"Return on equity is the product of the three: 8.0% x 1.25 x 2.0 = 20.0%. The first two multiply to return on assets of 10.0%, which leverage then doubles.","stem":"A company has a net profit margin of 8.0%, an asset turnover of 1.25 times and a financial leverage ratio of 2.0. Its return on equity is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":true,"choices":["USD490m","USD550m","USD710m"],"distractors":["This has the working capital signs the wrong way round, subtracting the payables increase and adding the receivables increase.","","This adds both working capital movements rather than netting the cash consumed by receivables."],"id":"fs-009","ref":"Cash flow from operations, indirect method","solution":"Add back the non-cash charge and adjust for working capital: 500 + 100 - 80 + 30 = USD550m. A rise in receivables consumes cash, and a rise in payables releases it.","stem":"A company reports net income of USD500m, depreciation of USD100m, an increase in receivables of USD80m and an increase in payables of USD30m. Using the indirect method, cash flow from operations is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["an operating outflow only.","either an operating or a financing outflow.","a financing outflow only."],"distractors":["This is the US GAAP treatment, not the IFRS one.","","Financing is one permitted IFRS choice, but not the only one."],"id":"fs-010","ref":"Classification of interest paid","solution":"IFRS allows interest paid to sit in either operating or financing activities, provided the choice is applied consistently and disclosed. US GAAP requires it in operating activities, which is a common source of non-comparability between the two frameworks.","stem":"Under IFRS, interest paid on borrowings is most likely reported in the cash flow statement as:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["accounting profit exceeds taxable income because depreciation is accelerated for tax purposes.","taxable income exceeds accounting profit because revenue is taxed before it is recognised.","the company carries forward unused tax losses to future periods."],"distractors":["","Paying tax ahead of recognising the revenue produces a deferred tax asset, because tax relief has effectively been taken early.","Unused tax losses give rise to a deferred tax asset, subject to a judgement about whether future profits will absorb them."],"id":"fs-011","ref":"Deferred tax liability","solution":"Claiming faster depreciation for tax than for reporting defers tax into later periods, so tax paid now is lower than the accounting charge and the difference sits as a liability. The timing difference reverses as the asset ages.","stem":"A deferred tax liability most likely arises when:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["leave total assets and total liabilities unchanged, with the rent recognised as incurred.","increase both total assets and total liabilities.","increase total liabilities and reduce equity by the present value of the payments."],"distractors":["Keeping leases off balance sheet and expensing the rent is the treatment IFRS 16 removed for lessees.","","Equity is unaffected at inception, because the asset recognised matches the liability."],"id":"fs-012","ref":"Lessee accounting","solution":"The IFRS lessee model recognises a right-of-use asset and a corresponding lease liability for essentially all leases. Both sides of the balance sheet grow, so leverage ratios deteriorate even though nothing about the business has changed.","stem":"A company with no leases on its balance sheet signs a five-year lease for its head office. Under the IFRS lessee model, the effect at inception is most likely to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["prohibit any reversal, so the written-down amount becomes the new cost.","permit a reversal, limited to the amount of the original write-down.","permit a write-up to fair value, with any excess over cost recognised in equity."],"distractors":["This is the US GAAP position. Under IFRS the write-down is not permanent.","","Inventory is never carried above cost under either framework, and the reversal runs through profit or loss rather than equity."],"id":"fs-013","ref":"Inventory measurement","solution":"IFRS permits a reversal of a previous inventory write-down where net realisable value recovers, capped at the original write-down so the carrying amount never exceeds cost. US GAAP prohibits reversal, which is a frequent difference between the two.","stem":"Under IFRS, inventory is measured at the lower of cost and net realisable value. Where a previous write-down is followed by a recovery in value, the treatment is most likely to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["1,075,000","1,225,000","1,300,000"],"distractors":["","This weights the new shares by three quarters of the year rather than one quarter.","This is the closing share count, which ignores that the new shares were absent for nine months."],"id":"fs-014","ref":"Weighted average shares outstanding","solution":"The new shares were outstanding for three of the twelve months: 1,000,000 + 300,000 x 3/12 = 1,075,000. Weighting by time is what stops a late issue inflating the denominator for the whole year.","stem":"A company begins the year with 1,000,000 shares outstanding and issues a further 300,000 shares on 1 October. Its weighted average number of shares outstanding for the year is closest to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["USD2.14","USD2.25","USD2.38"],"distractors":["","This is basic earnings per share, 9.0m / 4.0m, before the options are counted.","This omits the preferred dividend, dividing 10.0m by 4.2m. Preferred dividends are not available to ordinary shareholders."],"id":"fs-015","ref":"Diluted earnings per share","solution":"Earnings available to ordinary shareholders is 10.0 - 1.0 = USD9.0m. Diluted earnings per share divides that by the enlarged share count: 9.0m / 4.2m = USD2.14.","stem":"A company reports net income of USD10.0m, pays preferred dividends of USD1.0m, and has 4,000,000 weighted average ordinary shares outstanding. Employee options in the money would add 200,000 shares. Diluted earnings per share is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["total assets.","revenue.","the prior year's figure for the same line."],"distractors":["This is the convention for a common-size balance sheet rather than an income statement.","","This describes horizontal or trend analysis, which compares a company with its own past rather than with another company."],"id":"fs-016","ref":"Common-size analysis","solution":"A vertical common-size income statement states each line as a percentage of revenue, which strips out scale and makes margin structures directly comparable. Balance sheet items are commonly scaled by total assets instead.","stem":"An analyst builds a common-size income statement to compare two companies of very different size. Each line is most appropriately expressed as a percentage of:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["a normal consequence of growth, since receivables rise with sales.","a warning sign, because earnings are increasingly supported by accruals rather than cash.","evidence of conservative accounting, because revenue is recognised before cash is collected."],"distractors":["Receivables rising in line with revenue would be unremarkable. Rising much faster, while operating cash flow falls, is the opposite of unremarkable.","","Recognising revenue ahead of collection is aggressive rather than conservative, and it is what produces the accrual build in the first place."],"id":"fs-017","ref":"Quality of earnings","solution":"A widening gap between accounting earnings and operating cash flow means a growing share of profit sits in accruals. Receivables outrunning revenue points to either weakening collection or revenue recognised on terms that may not convert, and both reduce the reliability of reported earnings.","stem":"Over three years a company reports steadily rising net income while cash flow from operations falls and receivables grow much faster than revenue. This combination is best described as:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["leave the carrying amount unchanged, because impairments may not be reversed.","reverse the impairment, up to what the carrying amount would have been without it.","revalue the plant to its recoverable amount, recognising the gain in other comprehensive income."],"distractors":["This is the US GAAP treatment, and it applies to goodwill under IFRS, but not to a plant.","","The reversal runs through profit or loss and is capped at the original carrying amount path, rather than being a revaluation to recoverable amount."],"id":"fs-018","ref":"Impairment reversal","solution":"IFRS requires an impairment on a long-lived asset other than goodwill to be reversed where the recoverable amount recovers, capped at the depreciated carrying amount that would have applied had no impairment been taken. US GAAP prohibits reversal.","stem":"A company recognised an impairment loss on a manufacturing plant two years ago. Conditions have since improved and the recoverable amount now exceeds the carrying amount. Under IFRS the company is most likely required to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["amortised cost, with interest recognised over the holding period.","fair value, with changes recognised in profit or loss.","the lower of cost and market value, with declines recognised in profit or loss."],"distractors":["Amortised cost applies to debt held for its contractual cash flows, and equities have no such contractual stream.","","Lower of cost and market is an inventory convention, not a measurement basis for financial assets under IFRS."],"id":"fs-019","ref":"Measurement of financial assets","solution":"Equities held for trading are measured at fair value with gains and losses running through profit or loss, so reported earnings move with the market. Amortised cost is reserved for debt instruments held to collect contractual cash flows.","stem":"A company holds a portfolio of listed equities that it intends to trade actively. Under IFRS these holdings are most appropriately measured at:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["over time, as the performance obligation is satisfied.","at the point the bridge is handed over to the customer.","as cash instalments are received from the customer."],"distractors":["","Recognising everything on handover would be right only where control passes at a single point in time.","The timing of cash receipts does not drive recognition under accrual accounting."],"id":"fs-020","ref":"Revenue recognition","solution":"Where control transfers over time and progress can be measured reliably, revenue is recognised as the performance obligation is satisfied rather than waiting for completion. This matches revenue to the periods in which the work is done.","stem":"A contractor agrees to build a bridge over three years for a fixed price, and can reliably measure progress toward completion. Revenue is most appropriately recognised:"},{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["USD475m","USD505m","USD535m"],"distractors":["","This omits the dividend, which is a distribution out of retained earnings.","This adds the dividend rather than deducting it."],"id":"fs-021","ref":"Retained earnings as the link between statements","solution":"Closing retained earnings is opening retained earnings plus net income less dividends: 420 + 85 - 30 = USD475m. This is the line that ties the income statement to the balance sheet.","stem":"A company opens the year with retained earnings of USD420m, reports net income of USD85m and pays dividends of USD30m. Closing retained earnings is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["both reported net income and total cash flow unchanged.","reported net income different but total cash flow unchanged.","both reported net income and total cash flow different."],"distractors":["Net income does differ, which is the whole reason the choice matters to reported results.","","Absent a tax effect, cash paid for inventory is the same under either assumption."],"id":"fs-022","ref":"Inventory cost flow and cash flow","solution":"The cost flow assumption changes which costs are charged to the income statement, so reported profit differs. It does not change what was paid to suppliers, so cash flow is unaffected. In practice the choice can move cash indirectly, through the tax bill.","stem":"Ignoring any tax effect, a company's choice between LIFO and FIFO in a period of rising prices will most likely leave:"}],"pm":[{"answer":1,"calc":true,"choices":["8.50%","9.50%","10.40%"],"distractors":["This omits the beta adjustment, simply adding the premium to the risk-free rate.","","This applies beta to the sum of the risk-free rate and the premium, 1.20 x 8.5%, rather than to the premium alone."],"id":"pm-001","ref":"Capital asset pricing model","solution":"Required return is the risk-free rate plus beta multiplied by the equity risk premium: 3.5% + 1.20 x 5.0% = 3.5% + 6.0% = 9.50%.","stem":"The risk-free rate is 3.5%, the equity risk premium is 5.0% and a share has a beta of 1.20. The required return on the share under the capital asset pricing model is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":true,"choices":["12.65%","13.74%","16.00%"],"distractors":["This assumes zero correlation, dropping the covariance term: the square root of 160.","","This is the weighted average of the two standard deviations, which holds only at a correlation of exactly one."],"id":"pm-002","ref":"Two asset portfolio standard deviation","solution":"Variance is (0.6^2 x 400) + (0.4^2 x 100) + (2 x 0.6 x 0.4 x 0.30 x 20 x 10) = 144 + 16 + 28.8 = 188.8. The square root is 13.74%. It sits below the weighted average because correlation is less than one.","stem":"A portfolio holds 60% in an asset with a standard deviation of 20% and 40% in an asset with a standard deviation of 10%. The correlation between them is 0.30. The portfolio standard deviation is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["objectives.","constraints.","measures of risk tolerance."],"distractors":["Objectives cover only risk and return. Liquidity and horizon shape what the portfolio may do, rather than what it aims to achieve.","","Time horizon and liquidity influence how much risk a client can bear, but they are recorded as constraints. Risk tolerance is captured within the risk objective."],"id":"pm-003","ref":"Investment policy statement","solution":"An investment policy statement separates objectives, which are return and risk, from constraints, which are liquidity, time horizon, tax, legal and regulatory factors, and any unique circumstances. Liquidity and time horizon belong to the second group.","stem":"In an investment policy statement, a client's liquidity requirement and time horizon are most appropriately classified as:"},{"answer":1,"calc":true,"choices":["0.20","0.60","0.80"],"distractors":["This divides the risk-free rate by the standard deviation, or otherwise omits the portfolio return.","","This divides the total return rather than the excess return by standard deviation, 12.0 / 15.0."],"id":"pm-004","ref":"Sharpe ratio","solution":"The Sharpe ratio is excess return over total risk: (12.0 - 3.0) / 15.0 = 0.60. Using total risk rather than beta is what makes it the right measure for a portfolio that is not fully diversified.","stem":"A portfolio returns 12.0% with a standard deviation of 15.0%. The risk-free rate is 3.0%. Its Sharpe ratio is closest to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["1.04","1.10","2.20"],"distractors":["","This is the simple average of the two betas, which would apply only at equal weights.","This sums the two betas without weighting them."],"id":"pm-005","ref":"Portfolio beta","solution":"Beta is additive in weights: 0.40 x 1.40 + 0.60 x 0.80 = 0.56 + 0.48 = 1.04. Unlike standard deviation, no correlation term is needed, because beta already measures only the systematic component.","stem":"A portfolio holds 40% in a fund with a beta of 1.40 and 60% in a fund with a beta of 0.80. The portfolio beta is closest to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["0.075","0.100","0.108"],"distractors":["","This divides the excess return by 0.9, or otherwise misapplies the beta.","This divides the total return by beta rather than the excess return, 0.12 / 1.20 = 0.10, or multiplies instead of dividing."],"id":"pm-006","ref":"Treynor ratio","solution":"The Treynor ratio divides excess return by beta: (0.12 - 0.03) / 1.20 = 0.075. It is the appropriate measure where the portfolio is one component of a larger diversified holding, since only systematic risk then matters.","stem":"A portfolio returns 12.0% and has a beta of 1.20. The risk-free rate is 3.0%. Its Treynor ratio is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["total risk, as measured by the standard deviation of returns.","systematic risk only, as measured by beta.","unsystematic risk only, since systematic risk cannot be avoided."],"distractors":["Total risk includes the diversifiable part, which earns no premium.","","This has it backwards. Systematic risk is the part that cannot be diversified away, which is precisely why it is priced."],"id":"pm-007","ref":"Systematic and unsystematic risk","solution":"Unsystematic risk can be diversified away at no cost, so the market does not pay for bearing it. What remains after diversification is exposure to the market itself, and beta measures that.","stem":"According to the capital asset pricing model, an investor holding a well-diversified portfolio is most likely compensated for:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["undervalued, because it offers more return than its beta requires.","overvalued, because it carries more risk than the market will reward.","fairly valued, since the security market line describes an average rather than a boundary."],"distractors":["","Plotting above the line means better reward for the same beta, which is the definition of attractive rather than overvalued.","The line is the equilibrium relationship under the model, so a point off it is a mispricing rather than ordinary dispersion."],"id":"pm-008","ref":"The security market line","solution":"The security market line gives the return required for a given beta. A share expected to return more than that is cheap: buying pressure would lift its price and pull the expected return back down to the line.","stem":"A share's expected return plots above the security market line. Assuming the model holds, the share is most likely:"},{"answer":2,"calc":false,"choices":["+1.0","0.0","-1.0"],"distractors":["At a correlation of one there is no diversification benefit at all: portfolio standard deviation is the weighted average.","Zero correlation reduces risk substantially but not as far as negative correlation does.",""],"id":"pm-009","ref":"Correlation and diversification","solution":"The covariance term in portfolio variance falls as correlation falls, so the lower the correlation the greater the diversification benefit. At a correlation of negative one with equal standard deviations and equal weights, the two offset exactly and portfolio risk goes to zero.","stem":"Two assets have equal standard deviations and are combined in equal weights. The combination that produces the greatest reduction in portfolio standard deviation is most likely a correlation of:"},{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["12.6%","15.0%","18.0%"],"distractors":["","This averages the two standard deviations in some fashion, but the risk-free asset contributes no risk at all.","This is the standard deviation of the risky portfolio alone, ignoring the 30% held risk free."],"id":"pm-010","ref":"The capital allocation line","solution":"The risk-free asset has no volatility and no covariance with anything, so portfolio standard deviation is simply the risky weight times the risky standard deviation: 0.70 x 18.0% = 12.6%. That linearity is what makes the capital allocation line a straight line.","stem":"An investor puts 70% of her money in a risky portfolio with an expected return of 11.0% and a standard deviation of 18.0%, and 30% in the risk-free asset at 3.0%. The standard deviation of the combined holding is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["a relative risk objective.","an absolute risk objective.","a liquidity constraint."],"distractors":["A relative objective is stated against a benchmark, such as a limit on tracking error.","","A liquidity constraint concerns the need to withdraw cash at particular times, which is a different requirement."],"id":"pm-011","ref":"Absolute and relative risk objectives","solution":"An absolute risk objective is expressed in terms of the portfolio's own value, without reference to a benchmark. A relative objective would be framed as tracking error or as underperformance against an index.","stem":"A client states that the portfolio must not fall in value by more than 8% in any twelve-month period. This is best described as:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["a longer time horizon and a greater tolerance for illiquidity.","a shorter time horizon and a lower tolerance for illiquidity.","a similar horizon, since both exist to fund payments indefinitely."],"distractors":["","This describes the mature pension scheme rather than the endowment.","Both fund ongoing payments, but a closed or mature scheme has a defined and shortening liability profile while an endowment does not."],"id":"pm-012","ref":"Institutional investor characteristics","solution":"An endowment is typically managed in perpetuity with a modest annual spending requirement, so it can accept illiquid holdings. A scheme paying pensions to a mostly retired membership faces heavy near-term outflows and a shortening horizon.","stem":"Compared with a defined benefit pension scheme whose membership is largely retired, a large university endowment is most likely to have:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["reduce expected return, since the best performing asset is being trimmed.","restore the risk profile the client agreed to.","be avoidable, because the drift reflects a genuine improvement in equity prospects."],"distractors":["Trimming a winner may or may not cost return. The purpose of rebalancing is not to forecast, so this frames the decision as something it is not.","","Rising prices are not by themselves evidence of improved prospects. Treating drift as a view means the allocation is being changed by default rather than by decision."],"id":"pm-013","ref":"Strategic asset allocation and rebalancing","solution":"The strategic allocation encodes the risk the client agreed to bear. Drift changes that risk without anyone deciding to change it, so rebalancing is a risk control rather than a market call.","stem":"Equity markets rise sharply and a portfolio's equity weight drifts above its strategic target. Rebalancing back to target will most likely:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["loss aversion, reinforced by anchoring on the purchase price.","overconfidence in his ability to forecast.","a rational tax-management decision."],"distractors":["","Overconfidence shows up as excessive trading and unduly narrow forecast ranges, rather than as refusing to close a loser.","Realising a loss usually creates a tax benefit, so tax management would argue for selling rather than holding."],"id":"pm-014","ref":"Behavioural biases","solution":"Unwillingness to realise a loss, combined with treating the entry price as the reference point that determines when to act, is loss aversion working with anchoring. The purchase price is information about the past, not about the investment's prospects.","stem":"An investor holds a losing position well past the point at which the original thesis has broken, saying that he will sell once it returns to his purchase price. This behaviour is best described as:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["intraday dealing at prices set in the market.","dealing at the net asset value struck at the end of each day.","a guarantee that the market price equals net asset value."],"distractors":["","This is the mutual fund mechanism, and it is precisely what an exchange traded fund does not do.","No guarantee exists. Creation and redemption by authorised participants keeps the price close to net asset value, but premiums and discounts do occur."],"id":"pm-015","ref":"Exchange traded funds","solution":"An exchange traded fund trades on an exchange throughout the day at whatever price buyers and sellers agree, which may sit slightly away from net asset value. A mutual fund deals only at the daily valuation point.","stem":"Compared with an open-ended mutual fund tracking the same index, an exchange traded fund most likely offers:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["the risk management function, which measures and monitors exposures.","senior governance, because it is a decision about objectives rather than measurement.","the portfolio managers, who are closest to the positions."],"distractors":["Measuring and monitoring is the risk function's role. Choosing how much risk to accept is not.","","Managers work within the tolerance. Letting each set their own would leave the organisation with no aggregate limit."],"id":"pm-016","ref":"Risk governance","solution":"Setting risk tolerance is a governance decision, because it trades off the chance of shortfall against the objectives the organisation exists to meet. The risk function then measures and reports against that tolerance, and managers operate inside it.","stem":"Within a risk management framework, deciding the level of risk the organisation is willing to accept in pursuit of its objectives is most appropriately the responsibility of:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["the maximum the portfolio can lose in a day is USD2m.","on 5% of days the loss is expected to exceed USD2m.","the portfolio is expected to lose USD2m on 5% of days."],"distractors":["Value at risk says nothing about the size of losses beyond the threshold, which is its best known weakness.","","The figure is a floor for the worst 5% of days, not the expected loss on those days. That expected figure is conditional value at risk."],"id":"pm-017","ref":"Value at risk","solution":"Value at risk is a threshold, not a worst case. A one-day 95% figure of USD2m says that on about one day in twenty the loss should be larger than that, and it is silent about how much larger.","stem":"A portfolio has a one-day 95% value at risk of USD2m. The most accurate interpretation is that:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["another portfolio offers a higher expected return at the same level of risk.","the portfolio is riskier than any portfolio on the frontier.","the portfolio holds too few assets to be measured against the frontier."],"distractors":["","A portfolio below the frontier may have low risk. Its failing is the return it earns for that risk, not the risk level itself.","The number of holdings is not what defines the comparison. Any portfolio can be plotted in risk and return space."],"id":"pm-018","ref":"The efficient frontier","solution":"The frontier is the set of portfolios with the highest expected return for each level of risk. Sitting below it means the same risk is being taken for less return, or equivalently the same return is being earned for more risk.","stem":"A portfolio lies below the efficient frontier. This is most accurately described as meaning that:"}],"quant":[{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["money-weighted rate of return, because it reflects the actual growth of invested capital.","time-weighted rate of return, because it is not distorted by the size and timing of external cash flows.","internal rate of return, because it solves for the single rate that equates inflows and outflows."],"distractors":["This measures the investor's experience, which is the right question for a client report and the wrong one for ranking a manager who did not choose the cash flow dates.","","The internal rate of return is the money-weighted return under another name, so it carries the same timing distortion."],"id":"qm-001","ref":"Time-weighted and money-weighted return","solution":"A time-weighted return chains sub-period returns, so it measures the return on a unit of currency left in the fund throughout. That is the right basis for comparing managers who do not control when investors contribute or redeem. A money-weighted return answers a different question: what the investor actually earned given their own timing.","stem":"A manager runs a pooled fund into which investors contribute and from which they redeem at their own discretion. For the purpose of comparing this manager's performance against a peer group, the most appropriate return measure is the:"},{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["11.33%","12.00%","12.75%"],"distractors":["","This is the holding period return itself, before converting to a continuously compounded basis.","This is exp(0.12) - 1, which converts in the wrong direction."],"id":"qm-002","ref":"Continuously compounded return","solution":"The holding period return is (54.00 - 50.00 + 2.00) / 50.00 = 12.00%. The continuously compounded return is the natural log of one plus that figure: ln(1.12) = 11.33%. For a positive return the continuously compounded figure is always the smaller of the two.","stem":"An investor buys a share for USD50.00, receives a dividend of USD2.00 at the end of the year, and sells the share for USD54.00. The continuously compounded annual return is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":true,"choices":["0.25","2.50","25.00"],"distractors":["This divides by the sample standard deviation rather than the standard error, omitting the square root of the sample size.","","This divides by a standard error expressed in the wrong units, inflating the statistic tenfold."],"id":"qm-003","ref":"Test statistic for a mean","solution":"The test statistic is the difference between the sample mean and the hypothesised mean divided by the standard error. The standard error is 2.0% / sqrt(100) = 0.2%, so the statistic is (6.5 - 6.0) / 0.2 = 2.50. Against a two-tailed critical value of about 1.98 at the 5% level, the null would be rejected.","stem":"A sample of 100 monthly observations has a mean of 6.5% and a standard deviation of 2.0%. An analyst tests the hypothesis that the population mean equals 6.0%. The value of the test statistic is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":true,"choices":["6.00%","6.14%","6.18%"],"distractors":["This is the stated rate, which ignores compounding within the year.","","This is the continuously compounded equivalent, exp(0.06) - 1, which is the limit as compounding frequency rises without bound rather than the quarterly figure."],"id":"qm-004","ref":"Effective annual rate","solution":"The periodic rate is 6.0% / 4 = 1.5%, so the effective annual rate is (1.015)^4 - 1 = 6.14%. Compounding within the year lifts the effective rate above the stated rate.","stem":"A loan carries a stated annual rate of 6.0% compounded quarterly. Its effective annual rate is closest to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["USD36,800","USD39,008","USD50,000"],"distractors":["","This is the annuity due value, 36,800 x 1.06, which assumes the first payment arrives today rather than in a year.","This is the sum of the undiscounted payments."],"id":"qm-005","ref":"Present value of an ordinary annuity","solution":"The annuity factor is [1 - (1.06)^-10] / 0.06 = 7.3601, so the present value is 5,000 x 7.3601 = USD36,800. The payments arrive at each year end, which makes this an ordinary annuity.","stem":"An investment pays USD5,000 at the end of each year for ten years. At a discount rate of 6.0%, the present value of the payments is closest to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["negative 13.4%","0.0%","negative 25.0%"],"distractors":["","This is the arithmetic mean of the two returns. It always exceeds the geometric mean where returns vary, and it does not tie back to ending wealth.","This is the cumulative two-year return rather than the annual rate."],"id":"qm-006","ref":"Geometric and arithmetic mean return","solution":"Two currency units become 1.50 then 0.75, so the cumulative return is negative 25% over two years. The compound annual rate is the geometric mean: sqrt(1.50 x 0.50) - 1 = sqrt(0.75) - 1 = negative 13.4%. The arithmetic mean of 0% describes neither year's outcome nor the ending wealth.","stem":"A portfolio returns positive 50% in one year and negative 50% in the next. The compound annual rate of return over the two years is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":true,"choices":["0.67","1.50","4.00"],"distractors":["This inverts the ratio, dividing the mean by the standard deviation, which is a return-per-unit-of-risk measure rather than the coefficient of variation.","","This subtracts rather than divides, or otherwise mixes the two figures. The measure is a simple ratio."],"id":"qm-007","ref":"Coefficient of variation","solution":"The coefficient of variation is the standard deviation divided by the mean: 12.0 / 8.0 = 1.50. It measures dispersion per unit of return, so a lower figure is preferred when comparing investments.","stem":"A fund has an average annual return of 8.0% and a standard deviation of returns of 12.0%. Its coefficient of variation is closest to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["9.02% to 10.98%","9.50% to 10.50%","2.16% to 17.84%"],"distractors":["","This uses one standard error either side rather than 1.96 of them, which gives roughly a 68% interval.","This uses the sample standard deviation in place of the standard error, so the interval describes the spread of individual observations rather than the precision of the mean."],"id":"qm-008","ref":"Confidence interval for a mean","solution":"The standard error is 4.0% / sqrt(64) = 0.5%. The interval is 10.0% plus or minus 1.96 x 0.5% = 0.98%, so 9.02% to 10.98%.","stem":"A sample of 64 observations has a mean return of 10.0% and a standard deviation of 4.0%. Using a critical value of 1.96, the 95% confidence interval for the population mean is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":false,"choices":["a Type I error.","a Type II error.","the power of the test."],"distractors":["This is rejecting a true null. Its probability is the significance level the researcher sets.","","Power is the probability of correctly rejecting a false null, so it is one minus the probability of a Type II error rather than the error itself."],"id":"qm-009","ref":"Type I and Type II error","solution":"A Type II error is failing to reject a false null. A Type I error is the opposite: rejecting a null that is true, the probability of which is the chosen level of significance.","stem":"A researcher fails to reject a null hypothesis that is in fact false. This outcome is best described as:"},{"answer":0,"calc":true,"choices":["9.5%","12.5%","25.0%"],"distractors":["","This is the simple average of the two conditional probabilities, which ignores that the recession scenario carries only 30% weight.","This adds the two conditional probabilities. Probabilities of mutually exclusive scenarios are weighted, not summed."],"id":"qm-010","ref":"Total probability","solution":"Weight each conditional probability by the probability of the scenario: 0.30 x 0.20 + 0.70 x 0.05 = 0.060 + 0.035 = 0.095, or 9.5%.","stem":"An analyst assigns a 30% probability to a recession next year. She estimates the probability that a bond defaults at 20% if a recession occurs and 5% if it does not. The unconditional probability of default is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":true,"choices":["7.50%","10.61%","12.25%"],"distractors":["This is the probability-weighted absolute deviation rather than the square root of the weighted squared deviations.","","This takes the range of outcomes rather than the dispersion around the mean."],"id":"qm-011","ref":"Standard deviation of a discrete distribution","solution":"The expected return is 0.25(20) + 0.50(5) + 0.25(-10) = 5.0%. Deviations are 15, 0 and negative 15, so the variance is 0.25(225) + 0.50(0) + 0.25(225) = 112.5 and the standard deviation is sqrt(112.5) = 10.61%.","stem":"A holding returns positive 20% with probability 0.25, positive 5% with probability 0.50, and negative 10% with probability 0.25. The standard deviation of the return is closest to:"},{"answer":1,"calc":true,"choices":["2.3%","15.9%","34.1%"],"distractors":["This is the area beyond two standard deviations, which would correspond to a return below negative 16%.","","This is the area between the mean and one standard deviation below it, not the tail beyond it."],"id":"qm-012","ref":"Normal distribution probability","solution":"The z-score is (-4.0 - 8.0) / 12.0 = -1.00, so the question asks for the area more than one standard deviation below the mean. For a normal distribution that is about 15.9%.","stem":"Annual returns on a portfolio are approximately normally distributed with a mean of 8.0% and a standard deviation of 12.0%. The probability of a return below negative 4.0% in a given year is closest to:"},{"answer":0,"calc":false,"choices":["halve.","fall to one quarter of its former value.","be unchanged, because the population standard deviation has not changed."],"distractors":["","This assumes the standard error falls in proportion to the sample size. It falls with the square root, which is why large gains in precision get expensive.","The population standard deviation is indeed unchanged, but the standard error also depends on sample size, and that is what moved."],"id":"qm-013","ref":"Standard error and sample size","solution":"The standard error is the population standard deviation divided by the square root of the sample size. Quadrupling the sample doubles the square root, so the standard error halves. Precision improves with the square root of sample size, not in proportion to it.","stem":"An analyst increases her sample from 100 observations to 400, drawn from the same population. The standard error of the sample mean will most likely:"},{"answer":2,"calc":true,"choices":["0.13","0.36","0.60"],"distractors":["This squares the coefficient of determination rather than taking its square root.","This reports the coefficient of determination itself, which is the share of variation explained rather than the correlation.",""],"id":"qm-014","ref":"Coefficient of determination","solution":"In a simple linear regression the coefficient of determination is the square of the correlation, so the correlation is sqrt(0.36) = 0.60. The positive slope settles the sign, since the square root alone leaves it ambiguous.","stem":"A simple linear regression of a fund's returns on its benchmark's returns produces a coefficient of determination of 0.36 and a positive slope coefficient. The correlation between the two return series is closest to:"}]}};
